WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.952 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKER]

00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:02.928 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAK]

00:00:02.928 --> 00:00:04.392 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKIN]

00:00:04.392 --> 00:00:11.192 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.192 --> 00:00:12.900 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm
going to start today

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.880 align:middle line:84%
with a little bit of
a review of something

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.690 align:middle line:84%
that we talked about
sort of earlier

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.540 align:middle line:84%
when we were doing syntax
because it came in handy.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.520 align:middle line:84%
At a certain point, you
guys were pushing me

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.190 align:middle line:84%
with questions about
the fine details

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.300 align:middle line:90%
of syntactic structures.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.520 align:middle line:84%
And I wasn't able to
answer your questions

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.400 align:middle line:84%
without making reference
to another kind of test

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.260 align:middle line:84%
for syntactic structure,
which was binding theory.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.200 align:middle line:84%
And so I showed you
binding theory back then,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.160 align:middle line:84%
but I'm going to go over it
again in a little more detail.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.660 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk about some things
that we didn't get a chance

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.530 align:middle line:90%
to talk about last time.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.470 align:middle line:90%
And then we'll go on from there.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.750 align:middle line:84%
Binding theory is
the theory that's

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.170 align:middle line:84%
meant to handle facts
like the facts that

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.230 align:middle line:84%
are on this slide,
namely, the fact that,

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.760 align:middle line:84%
if you say that these are
both grammatical sentences,

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.950 align:middle line:84%
but that they mean
different things.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.010 align:middle line:84%
The first one, "Susan
likes herself,"

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.340 align:middle line:84%
you could paraphrase
as "Susan likes Susan."

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.460 align:middle line:90%
"Herself" has to refer to Susan.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.260 align:middle line:84%
And possibly relatedly,
"Susan likes her."

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.030 align:middle line:90%
You don't know who "her" is.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.300 align:middle line:84%
But you do one thing, which
is that it is not Susan.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.550 align:middle line:84%
That's not a general
fact about "her."

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.020 align:middle line:84%
It's a fact about
"her" in this sentence.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.720 align:middle line:84%
So if I said "I like
her," then "her"

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.060 align:middle line:84%
could be Susan,
whoever Susan is.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.820 align:middle line:84%
But if it's "Susan likes
her," then that "her"

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.290 align:middle line:84%
cannot be Susan, or at least not
the same Susan as the subject,

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:34.620 align:middle line:90%
yeah?

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.090 align:middle line:84%
Binding theory is meant to
capture facts like this.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:44.460 align:middle line:84%
And these starred sentences
incorporate some of the things

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.710 align:middle line:90%
that I just said, not only--

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.852 align:middle line:84%
so these little letters,
these subscripts

00:01:48.852 --> 00:01:50.310 align:middle line:84%
that people put on
these words, are

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.880 align:middle line:84%
meant to indicate things about
what can corefer with what.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.770 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan likes herself,"
the first sentence, is OK.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.660 align:middle line:84%
And the third
sentence is starred.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.480 align:middle line:84%
Because if you say the
string "Susan likes herself,"

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.630 align:middle line:84%
then "Susan" and "herself" have
to refer to the same person.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.139 align:middle line:84%
And we indicate that by giving
them the same subscript.

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:14.520 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan" sub a likes
"herself" sub a, that's OK.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.970 align:middle line:84%
But "Susan" sub a likes
"herself" sub b is not OK.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.433 align:middle line:84%
The two subscripts
have to be the same.

00:02:20.433 --> 00:02:22.350 align:middle line:84%
And with a pronoun, it's
the other way around.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.735 align:middle line:90%
They have to be different.

00:02:23.735 --> 00:02:25.103 align:middle line:90%
OK so far?

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:27.270 align:middle line:84%
Nobody is sitting there
quietly thinking, "No, wait.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.180 align:middle line:84%
I say 'Susan likes her,' and I
mean 'Susan likes herself' all

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.150 align:middle line:90%
the time.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.497 align:middle line:84%
No wonder nobody
ever understands me."

00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:34.830 align:middle line:90%
Nobody's having that experience?

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.800 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.940 align:middle line:84%
So, you know, looking at this
without thinking about it

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.790 align:middle line:84%
too hard, we could think,
OK, maybe pronouns--

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.550 align:middle line:84%
we'll use "pronoun" to
refer to words like "her"--

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:51.660 align:middle line:84%
cannot corefer with anything in
the sentence, where "corefer"

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.600 align:middle line:90%
means refer to the same person.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.130 align:middle line:84%
And anaphors, which is a name
for fancy words like "herself,"

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.810 align:middle line:90%
must corefer with something.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.210 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan likes
herself, herself"

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.800 align:middle line:90%
needs to refer back to Susan.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.100 align:middle line:84%
You have to give
"herself" something for it

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.090 align:middle line:90%
to corefer with.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:15.520 align:middle line:90%


00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.650 align:middle line:84%
And when we did
binding theory before,

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.270 align:middle line:90%
we already got this far.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.430 align:middle line:84%
A word like "herself"
has this special property

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:25.450 align:middle line:84%
that it has to corefer with
something else in the sentence.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.950 align:middle line:84%
But interesting fact, it can't
just refer with anything.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.177 align:middle line:84%
And this is why
there's a theory.

00:03:32.177 --> 00:03:33.760 align:middle line:84%
You have to do some
work to figure out

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.930 align:middle line:84%
which kinds of things
it can refer to.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.680 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan likes
herself" is fine,

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.380 align:middle line:84%
but "Susan's father
likes herself,"

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:47.000 align:middle line:84%
where "herself" is
Susan, is no good.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.190 align:middle line:84%
One hopes that Susan's
father likes Susan,

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:51.020 align:middle line:84%
but this is not
the way to say it.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.360 align:middle line:84%
So "herself" can't
refer back to Susan

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.940 align:middle line:84%
in a sentence like
this, which knocks out

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.250 align:middle line:84%
some of the simplest,
easiest theories you might

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.630 align:middle line:84%
have had from the first batch of
sentences on the earlier slide.

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:04.340 align:middle line:84%
You might have thought,
well, you know,

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.110 align:middle line:84%
anaphors, they need to
refer back to something.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.610 align:middle line:84%
And you might even
have thought they

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.250 align:middle line:84%
have to refer back to something
that's earlier in the sentence.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.700 align:middle line:84%
They're kind of a pointer
that points back to something

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:14.492 align:middle line:90%
that's before them.

00:04:14.492 --> 00:04:15.908 align:middle line:84%
And what we're
seeing is that it's

00:04:15.908 --> 00:04:17.209 align:middle line:90%
more complicated than that.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:20.400 align:middle line:84%
They can't just
pick out anything.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.650 align:middle line:84%
There are some restrictions
on what they can refer to.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.232 align:middle line:84%
We have to understand
what those are.

00:04:24.232 --> 00:04:26.690 align:middle line:84%
There's a classic story, which
we've already been through--

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:28.130 align:middle line:90%
I'll just show it to you again--

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:31.970 align:middle line:84%
about why it's OK to say
"Susan must like herself,"

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:36.230 align:middle line:84%
but bad to say "Susan's
father must like herself."

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.083 align:middle line:84%
The classic story
goes like this.

00:04:38.083 --> 00:04:39.500 align:middle line:84%
What we're trying
to do is come up

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.620 align:middle line:84%
with a structural
relationship between "Susan"

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.220 align:middle line:84%
and "herself" that holds
in the tree in the left,

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.557 align:middle line:84%
but not in the
tree in the right.

00:04:48.557 --> 00:04:50.390 align:middle line:84%
We're talking about
structural relationships

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:54.500 align:middle line:84%
because, well, your first
hope, which might have been,

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.980 align:middle line:84%
yeah, "herself" has to refer to
something that's been mentioned

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:02.220 align:middle line:84%
before, the way you might have
hoped language was designed,

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.740 align:middle line:84%
there are no
languages like that.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:05.510 align:middle line:84%
There are, instead,
languages like this

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.760 align:middle line:84%
that apparently care
about trees when

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.230 align:middle line:84%
they're deciding
which things can

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:12.650 align:middle line:90%
refer to which other things.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:14.150 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going
to try to develop

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:17.330 align:middle line:84%
a structural distinction
between the relationship

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:20.047 align:middle line:84%
between "Susan" and "herself"
in the tree on the left

00:05:20.047 --> 00:05:22.130 align:middle line:84%
and the relationship between
"Susan" and "herself"

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:23.590 align:middle line:90%
in the tree on the right.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:25.340 align:middle line:84%
As I said, we've talked
about this before.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.280 align:middle line:90%
We're just reviewing it.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.430 align:middle line:84%
The story that people have
classically come up with

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.940 align:middle line:84%
makes use of the fact
that Susan is more deeply

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:38.090 align:middle line:84%
buried in the tree on the
right than she is on the left.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.405 align:middle line:84%
So there's a relation
called c-command.

00:05:40.405 --> 00:05:43.540 align:middle line:90%


00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:47.605 align:middle line:84%
It's a relation that can hold
between two nodes in a tree.

00:05:47.605 --> 00:05:52.600 align:middle line:84%
It says alpha c-commands beta if
every node that dominates alpha

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.790 align:middle line:90%
dominates beta.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:58.300 align:middle line:84%
So you c-command basically the
thing that you were merged with

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.450 align:middle line:84%
and everything that
that dominates.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:07.810 align:middle line:84%
So in this tree, the NP
"Susan" c-commands "herself."

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:09.340 align:middle line:84%
The way we find
that out is we ask,

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:12.190 align:middle line:84%
what are the nodes that dominate
the noun phrase "Susan"?

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.140 align:middle line:84%
And there's only one, that
TP that I've put up there.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:17.170 align:middle line:84%
And that TP also
dominates "herself,"

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:20.470 align:middle line:84%
and so "Susan"
c-commands "herself."

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:23.810 align:middle line:84%
Does "herself"
c-command "Susan"?

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:24.310 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.685 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, you're all shaking your
heads appropriately, right?

00:06:26.685 --> 00:06:28.350 align:middle line:84%
And the way to find
that out is to ask,

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.920 align:middle line:84%
what are the nodes
that dominate herself?

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.590 align:middle line:84%
And they include that TP,
but somebody name another one

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.380 align:middle line:90%
that doesn't dominate "Susan."

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:36.017 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: VP.

00:06:36.017 --> 00:06:37.100 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: VP, yeah.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.000 align:middle line:90%
Or T bar, yeah?

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.465 align:middle line:84%
So there are at least those
two which dominate "herself,"

00:06:41.465 --> 00:06:42.590 align:middle line:90%
but don't dominate "Susan."

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:44.390 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan" c-commands
"herself," but "herself"

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:45.590 align:middle line:90%
doesn't c-command "Susan."

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:49.460 align:middle line:84%
And what we're seeing
is that maybe an anaphor

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.110 align:middle line:84%
needs to refer to something
that c-commands it.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.860 align:middle line:84%
So in this sentence,
"Susan" must like herself,"

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.810 align:middle line:90%
"herself" is referring to Susan.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:59.240 align:middle line:84%
And "Susan" c-commands
"herself," as opposed

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.700 align:middle line:84%
to the other tree, "Susan's
father must like herself,"

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:03.855 align:middle line:90%
which is no good.

00:07:03.855 --> 00:07:04.730 align:middle line:90%
What's wrong with it?

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:07.430 align:middle line:84%
Well, what's wrong with
it is, on this story,

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:10.340 align:middle line:84%
there are multiple nodes
dominating "Susan."

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:13.070 align:middle line:90%
There's TP, but also NP and DP.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:15.500 align:middle line:84%
And some of those don't
dominate "herself."

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.920 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan" doesn't
c-command "herself."

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.925 align:middle line:84%
Binding theory
makes use of this.

00:07:21.925 --> 00:07:23.300 align:middle line:84%
Let me just call
your attention--

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.240 align:middle line:84%
maybe your attention is
already firmly fixed on this--

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.190 align:middle line:90%
to how weird this is.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:33.710 align:middle line:84%
I just alluded to this, but
let me just say it again.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:36.110 align:middle line:84%
I'm showing you a bunch
of facts about English.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:38.390 align:middle line:84%
And different languages
do have different anaphors

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.230 align:middle line:90%
that behave in different ways.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.350 align:middle line:84%
But here's something no language
ever does, as far as I know.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.020 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
in the world where

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:49.370 align:middle line:84%
an anaphor can refer to
anything that was said earlier.

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:53.432 align:middle line:84%
There are no languages in which
both "Susan likes herself"

00:07:53.432 --> 00:07:54.890 align:middle line:84%
and "Susan's father
likes herself,"

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:56.330 align:middle line:90%
in which they're both OK.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:58.130 align:middle line:84%
There just aren't any
languages like that

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.820 align:middle line:84%
in which the important thing
is does the noun precede

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:06.440 align:middle line:84%
the anaphor, which is, so
first of all, interesting.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.090 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any
languages like that.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:12.483 align:middle line:84%
But second of all, weirdly,
surprisingly, interesting,

00:08:12.483 --> 00:08:14.150 align:middle line:84%
let's get ourselves
in the frame of mind

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:16.100 align:middle line:84%
where we can be impressed
by a fact like that.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:19.760 align:middle line:84%
Because, look, imagine
what you were like when

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:20.960 align:middle line:90%
you were a baby, right?

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:21.560 align:middle line:90%
Here you are.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:22.850 align:middle line:90%
You've just been born.

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:24.830 align:middle line:84%
You're hearing
people speak, right?

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:27.040 align:middle line:84%
They're saying sentences
in this language that's

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:28.415 align:middle line:84%
going to be your
native language,

00:08:28.415 --> 00:08:29.480 align:middle line:90%
and you're trying to learn it.

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.340 align:middle line:84%
You're trying to figure
things out about it.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:34.010 align:middle line:84%
What's the one
thing that you know?

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.360 align:middle line:90%
You hear people talking.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.309 align:middle line:84%
And they're saying
things like-- here,

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:39.539 align:middle line:84%
I'll put a sentence on
the board in a language

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:40.789 align:middle line:90%
that I hope none of you speak.

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:48.530 align:middle line:90%


00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:50.510 align:middle line:90%
So here's a sentence.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:55.910 align:middle line:84%
You hear one of your parents
saying, [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]..

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:57.860 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they write it
down and show it to you.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:59.485 align:middle line:84%
So you know, for some
reason, that it's

00:08:59.485 --> 00:09:01.970 align:middle line:90%
1, 2, 3, 4, 5 words long.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:05.893 align:middle line:84%
What else do you know
about that sentence?

00:09:05.893 --> 00:09:07.560 align:middle line:84%
Do you know which
words in that sentence

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.940 align:middle line:90%
c-command which other words?

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.400 align:middle line:90%
Good lord, no.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:12.583 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:09:12.583 --> 00:09:14.250 align:middle line:84%
Do you know which
words in that sentence

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.490 align:middle line:90%
precede which other words?

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:17.600 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:20.975 align:middle line:84%
So you know that [NON-ENGLISH]
precedes [NON-ENGLISH]..

00:09:20.975 --> 00:09:22.405 align:middle line:90%
You know that.

00:09:22.405 --> 00:09:24.530 align:middle line:84%
But you don't know whether
[NON-ENGLISH] c-commands

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:25.890 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH] or not.

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:28.550 align:middle line:84%
So the one bit of information
that you have immediately

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:32.370 align:middle line:84%
from the get-go, which words
come before which other words,

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:34.610 align:middle line:90%
you ignore.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:36.920 align:middle line:84%
So languages don't ever
make use of that when

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:38.490 align:middle line:90%
they're doing binding theory.

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:44.000 align:middle line:84%
So you would have thought this
one sort of easy to access

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.410 align:middle line:84%
bit of information
that all of us

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:48.365 align:middle line:84%
have from day one,
no matter, you know--

00:09:48.365 --> 00:09:50.240 align:middle line:84%
don't know anything else
about your language.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:53.090 align:middle line:84%
You at least know which things
come before which other things.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:55.688 align:middle line:84%
Binding theory never,
ever cares about that.

00:09:55.688 --> 00:09:57.230 align:middle line:84%
It only cares about
c-commands, which

00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.120 align:middle line:84%
is this thing that you
have to know something

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.560 align:middle line:90%
in order to understand.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:03.550 align:middle line:90%


00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:05.890 align:middle line:84%
It's as though our
brains are designed

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.980 align:middle line:84%
to build language in
some ways and not others

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:14.620 align:middle line:84%
and to ignore certain
sources of data

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.148 align:middle line:90%
which are very, very fruitful.

00:10:16.148 --> 00:10:18.190 align:middle line:84%
You have lots and lots of
data about which things

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:20.868 align:middle line:84%
precede which other
things, but you don't care.

00:10:20.868 --> 00:10:21.910 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of interesting.

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:23.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:26.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If, for
some reason, we

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:29.823 align:middle line:84%
did care about the order of
the words that we're saying,

00:10:29.823 --> 00:10:30.990 align:middle line:90%
would that create ambiguity?

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:35.790 align:middle line:84%
Because some languages
like, German, half of it

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:38.108 align:middle line:84%
is head initial and
half is head final.

00:10:38.108 --> 00:10:40.035 align:middle line:90%
And so you'd be confused.

00:10:40.035 --> 00:10:42.410 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So which
things proceed and follow which

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:42.990 align:middle line:90%
other things?

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.990 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there are ambiguities
in binding theory.

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:47.032 align:middle line:84%
We've been talking about
simple sentences so far,

00:10:47.032 --> 00:10:50.090 align:middle line:84%
but you can say things like--
if anybody is wondering,

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:51.320 align:middle line:90%
this [NON-ENGLISH].

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:59.070 align:middle line:84%
It means "Is drinking
the man the water,"

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:01.130 align:middle line:84%
so "The man is
drinking the water."

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.218 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, I just told you
that so you wouldn't

00:11:03.218 --> 00:11:04.260 align:middle line:90%
have to worry about that.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:05.630 align:middle line:84%
There are ambiguities
in binding theory.

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.250 align:middle line:84%
I've been talking about
sentences where there aren't

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:08.708 align:middle line:84%
ambiguities, but
you can say things

00:11:08.708 --> 00:11:19.210 align:middle line:84%
like "Susan told
Stacy about herself,"

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:23.260 align:middle line:84%
where probably the
easiest reading for that

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:26.560 align:middle line:84%
is that Susan told
Stacy about Susan.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.025 align:middle line:84%
But if Stacy is
unclear about herself,

00:11:32.025 --> 00:11:34.150 align:middle line:84%
there's something Stacy
doesn't know about herself,

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:35.800 align:middle line:84%
Susan could tell
Stacy about herself

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:39.010 align:middle line:84%
and have that mean Susan told
Stacy about Stacy, I think.

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:40.220 align:middle line:90%
Am I making that up?

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:41.110 align:middle line:90%
I think that's true.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:42.730 align:middle line:90%
This is just ambiguous.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:43.700 align:middle line:90%
So here's a sentence.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.570 align:middle line:84%
So we're OK with
ambiguity usually.

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:48.142 align:middle line:84%
There are plenty
of places where,

00:11:48.142 --> 00:11:49.600 align:middle line:84%
if we were to draw
a tree for this,

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:51.700 align:middle line:84%
we'd want to draw a tree
where "Susan" and "Stacy"

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:53.500 align:middle line:90%
both c-command "herself."

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:56.050 align:middle line:84%
And so it has a choice
of which kind of thing

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:57.310 align:middle line:90%
it wants to refer to.

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:59.558 align:middle line:84%
All it cares about is
which thing c-commands it,

00:11:59.558 --> 00:12:01.600 align:middle line:84%
but it doesn't care about
which thing precedes it

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.250 align:middle line:90%
in any language on Earth.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.170 align:middle line:90%
It was kind of interesting.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:06.040 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, go.

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:08.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was also thinking
about you could have something

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:12.450 align:middle line:84%
like "Carrie likes
Mary herself."

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:16.710 align:middle line:84%
Maybe Mary played the evil
character on TV and people

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:18.210 align:middle line:84%
don't like-- she
likes Mary herself.

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:19.460 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Right, right.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:22.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But it's like, all the
other people, they hate Mary.

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:24.573 align:middle line:90%
But she, herself, she likes--

00:12:24.573 --> 00:12:25.740 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: She-- yeah.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.265 align:middle line:84%
You're raising-- actually, I'm
glad you asked that question

00:12:28.265 --> 00:12:30.640 align:middle line:84%
because it raises a good point,
which I should have made.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:34.920 align:middle line:84%
And so thank you for raising
it, which is that languages use

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:38.910 align:middle line:84%
"herself," "himself,"
"myself," all these "-self"

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:42.390 align:middle line:84%
kinds of doohickeys, for a
bunch of different things.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:46.290 align:middle line:84%
We're going to be concentrating
on the "herself" that

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.960 align:middle line:84%
sits where a noun phrase
would normally sit

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.590 align:middle line:84%
and refers back to
another noun phrase.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.780 align:middle line:84%
But there is this
other, sometimes called,

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.630 align:middle line:90%
an intensive use of reflexives.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:59.220 align:middle line:90%
And I can't spell today--

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:13.020 align:middle line:84%
intensive, where you say things
like "John, himself, fixed

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:16.980 align:middle line:84%
the computer," or,
for that matter,

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:20.380 align:middle line:84%
"John fixed the
computer himself,"

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:27.270 align:middle line:84%
where that means something
not so different from "John

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:29.010 align:middle line:90%
fixed the computer."

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:31.080 align:middle line:84%
It means something like
"John fixed the computer,

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.990 align:middle line:90%
and no one else did."

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:37.060 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan's father
must like herself."

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:40.230 align:middle line:84%
We were talking about things
that "herself" can refer to.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:42.168 align:middle line:84%
Or "Susan told Stacy
about herself."

00:13:42.168 --> 00:13:44.460 align:middle line:84%
We're talking about things
that "herself" can refer to.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.860 align:middle line:84%
This would be an OK sentence
if you changed "herself"

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:47.400 align:middle line:90%
to something else.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:48.608 align:middle line:90%
It would mean something else.

00:13:48.608 --> 00:13:52.080 align:middle line:84%
But "Susan told
Stacy about Mary,"

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.610 align:middle line:90%
that's also a sentence, right?

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.390 align:middle line:84%
So "herself" is
just a noun phrase,

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:57.060 align:middle line:84%
noun phrase with
special properties,

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:59.370 align:middle line:84%
that's sitting where
noun phrases go.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:02.500 align:middle line:84%
If you replaced "himself"
here with something else,

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:05.940 align:middle line:84%
so if it were "John,
Mary fixed the computer,"

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:09.450 align:middle line:84%
you would no longer
be speaking English.

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:12.345 align:middle line:84%
So this is a different
construction, the construction

00:14:12.345 --> 00:14:14.220 align:middle line:84%
that you're raising, a
really interesting one

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:17.790 align:middle line:84%
which I'm now going to
ignore for the rest of today.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.940 align:middle line:90%
But that's what this is.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:21.823 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:14:21.823 --> 00:14:23.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, I'm
bringing this again.

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:24.330 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, no.

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:25.090 align:middle line:90%
I'm ignoring you.

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:25.590 align:middle line:90%
No, sorry.

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:26.480 align:middle line:90%
Go ahead.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:27.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is that
a type of movement?

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:28.020 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry?

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is that another
example of movement?

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:30.510 align:middle line:90%
Or--

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:31.050 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Which one?

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.258 align:middle line:84%
Oh, the fact that himself
can be either here or here?

00:14:33.258 --> 00:14:34.412 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:14:34.412 --> 00:14:35.370 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Maybe.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:39.640 align:middle line:90%


00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:43.490 align:middle line:90%
Ah, we have to be careful.

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:47.770 align:middle line:84%
There are various kinds
of-- so himself, here, it's

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:49.570 align:middle line:90%
kind of like an adverb, right?

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:53.140 align:middle line:84%
It's modifying the way in
which he fixed the computer.

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:56.082 align:middle line:84%
And just as you can see
things like "John quickly"--

00:14:56.082 --> 00:14:57.790 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why I
switched to John here.

00:14:57.790 --> 00:14:59.170 align:middle line:90%
Let's make Susan do this, too.

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:03.220 align:middle line:90%


00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:06.010 align:middle line:84%
Just as you can say Susan
quickly fixed the computer,

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:09.850 align:middle line:84%
or Susan fixed the
computer quickly,

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:11.860 align:middle line:90%
you could do that with movement.

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:12.965 align:middle line:90%
Or you could decide--

00:15:12.965 --> 00:15:14.590 align:middle line:84%
we're learning
something about adverbs,

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:17.140 align:middle line:84%
that adverbs, because
it's an adjunct maybe,

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.270 align:middle line:84%
there are more options
about where it can go.

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:22.750 align:middle line:84%
The rules about where exactly
it goes are not as strict.

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:25.090 align:middle line:84%
The clean arguments
for movement are

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:28.300 align:middle line:84%
cases where we know for a fact
that something should be here,

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:31.150 align:middle line:90%
and yet it's over here.

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:33.910 align:middle line:90%
When we do "What did he devour?"

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:35.380 align:middle line:90%
that's what we're doing there.

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:36.130 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:37.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But doesn't the
"you" in that sentence kind

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.058 align:middle line:84%
of change depending on
where you put the "herself"?

00:15:40.058 --> 00:15:41.350 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, sometimes.

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:42.227 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:42.227 --> 00:15:43.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The first one,
you could have "herself"

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:45.827 align:middle line:84%
after Susan if you're
implying that it's surprising

00:15:45.827 --> 00:15:48.160 align:middle line:84%
that Susan, herself, was the
one who fixed the computer.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:48.760 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:50.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Or as you put it
at the end, it's you just

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:51.700 align:middle line:90%
say that it was Susan that--

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:52.660 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, so these might

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:53.870 align:middle line:90%
be different kinds of adverbs.

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:54.700 align:middle line:90%
That's a very good point.

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:55.970 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a very nice point.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:58.012 align:middle line:84%
So if I say "Susan, herself,
fixed the computer,"

00:15:58.012 --> 00:15:59.853 align:middle line:84%
we mean Susan is the
CEO of the company.

00:15:59.853 --> 00:16:02.270 align:middle line:84%
You would have expected her
to hire someone else to do it,

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:04.540 align:middle line:84%
but she did it
herself, whereas "Susan

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:08.290 align:middle line:84%
fixed the computer herself,"
it doesn't mean necessarily

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:09.860 align:middle line:84%
that it's surprising
that she did it.

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:12.340 align:middle line:84%
It just means that she did
it and no one else did.

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:13.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:15.670 align:middle line:84%
So they're related
meanings, but separate.

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:18.730 align:middle line:84%
So we may want to distinguish
two different types

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:19.930 align:middle line:90%
of intensive modification.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.267 align:middle line:84%
You're absolutely
right, nice point.

00:16:22.267 --> 00:16:23.850 align:middle line:84%
Other nice points
people wish to make?

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:24.350 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:27.428 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So to clarify, the
reason this tree is not valid

00:16:27.428 --> 00:16:32.220 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] is that "herself"
is looking for something to bind

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:32.720 align:middle line:90%
to.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:33.637 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:16:33.637 --> 00:16:37.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And so it needs to be
binded [INAUDIBLE] binds to NP.

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:39.820 align:middle line:84%
It binds "Susan," and it
binds "Susan's father."

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:41.410 align:middle line:84%
And it can't bind
to "Susan" because

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:42.742 align:middle line:90%
of the c-command constraint.

00:16:42.742 --> 00:16:45.747 align:middle line:84%
It can't bind to "Susan's
father" because "father"--

00:16:45.747 --> 00:16:47.330 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Because wrong gender.

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:47.570 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:48.050 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yup.

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:48.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] himself.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:49.645 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yup.

00:16:49.645 --> 00:16:51.740 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's it exactly.

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:53.690 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so the idea is--

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:56.390 align:middle line:84%
so we weren't asking why can't
it refer to Susan's father.

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:58.098 align:middle line:84%
You just asked that
question, and I think

00:16:58.098 --> 00:17:00.850 align:middle line:90%
you answered it perfectly.

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:02.600 align:middle line:84%
"Herself" can't refer
to "Susan's father,"

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:06.170 align:middle line:84%
which does c-command "herself,"
because Susan's father is male.

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:06.710 align:middle line:90%
Wake up.

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:09.380 align:middle line:90%


00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:11.329 align:middle line:84%
And it can't refer to
"Susan" because "Susan"

00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:14.995 align:middle line:90%
doesn't c-command it.

00:17:14.995 --> 00:17:15.810 align:middle line:90%
Is it back?

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:15.950 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:16.460 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:17.320 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:19.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So then to
clarify, if the sentence was,

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:21.750 align:middle line:84%
"Susan's father
must like himself,"

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:23.609 align:middle line:84%
that's fine because
"Susan's father"

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:24.962 align:middle line:90%
is the NP that's c-commanding.

00:17:24.962 --> 00:17:25.920 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Right.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:27.660 align:middle line:84%
And that's my intuition
about the sentence.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:28.243 align:middle line:90%
Is that yours?

00:17:28.243 --> 00:17:29.760 align:middle line:90%
And that would be OK.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:34.320 align:middle line:84%
It means Susan's father
has a healthy self-regard.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:35.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:36.360 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:39.600 align:middle line:84%
So binding theory, I introduced
you to binding theory

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.220 align:middle line:90%
as a test for structure, right?

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:45.450 align:middle line:84%
So once we are willing
to trust binding theory

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.580 align:middle line:84%
at least a little ways,
we can convince ourselves

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:49.680 align:middle line:84%
that we have a new
way of finding out

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:50.982 align:middle line:90%
things about structure.

00:17:50.982 --> 00:17:52.440 align:middle line:84%
We know we have
the right structure

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.898 align:middle line:84%
if we have the right c-command
relations to get the binding

00:17:54.898 --> 00:17:57.400 align:middle line:90%
relations that we seem to find.

00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:00.300 align:middle line:84%
So for example, when we're
thinking about sentences

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:03.122 align:middle line:84%
like this, "Susan told
Stacy about herself,"

00:18:03.122 --> 00:18:05.580 align:middle line:84%
we're going to need it to have
a structure in which "Susan"

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:07.680 align:middle line:84%
and "Stacy" both
c-command herself.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.200 align:middle line:84%
And so when we were talking
before about complicated

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:12.795 align:middle line:84%
verb phrases, binding
theory is the kind of thing

00:18:12.795 --> 00:18:14.670 align:middle line:84%
that people use as a
probe into the structure

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:16.910 align:middle line:90%
of complicated verb phrases.

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:19.353 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:18:19.353 --> 00:18:21.020 align:middle line:84%
All right, so one
part of binding theory

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:23.240 align:middle line:84%
is going to say
anaphors, these are words

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:26.210 align:middle line:90%
like "herself" or "myself"--

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:29.450 align:middle line:84%
in English, they all
end with "-self."

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:32.330 align:middle line:84%
They need to be
c-commanded by something

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.620 align:middle line:90%
that corefers with them.

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:36.582 align:middle line:90%
And then we have a short name.

00:18:36.582 --> 00:18:38.040 align:middle line:84%
I hope I do this
on the next slide.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:39.207 align:middle line:90%
I'm not sure if I do or not.

00:18:39.207 --> 00:18:42.650 align:middle line:84%
We have a short name for
to c-command something

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:45.750 align:middle line:84%
that you corefer with,
we call that bind.

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:51.110 align:middle line:84%
So we say that, in "Susan
told Stacy about herself,"

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:53.060 align:middle line:90%
"herself" needs to be bound.

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:55.460 align:middle line:84%
And there are two noun
phrases that could bind it,

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:57.367 align:middle line:90%
"Susan" and "Stacy."

00:18:57.367 --> 00:18:59.450 align:middle line:84%
And so we need a structure
for this in which those

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:01.850 align:middle line:84%
both c-command herself,
and either of them

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:03.830 align:middle line:90%
could corefer with herself.

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:05.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:09.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do all anaphors
necessarily end in "-self,"

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:12.590 align:middle line:84%
or are there cases where
pronouns act as anaphors?

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:15.740 align:middle line:84%
So for example, "John said
he went to the store,"

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:17.280 align:middle line:90%
where "he" is referring to John.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.280 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
that's a very nice point.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.960 align:middle line:84%
So, so far, you may have
noticed all of my examples

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.510 align:middle line:90%
have only been one clause long.

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:28.050 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to make
things more complicated

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:30.550 align:middle line:90%
in just a second.

00:19:30.550 --> 00:19:33.405 align:middle line:84%
But here, just to get started on
making things more complicated,

00:19:33.405 --> 00:19:35.530 align:middle line:84%
we've been talking about
the fact that you can say,

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:43.110 align:middle line:90%
"Susan must like herself."

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:45.100 align:middle line:84%
If we make this an
embedded clause,

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:51.870 align:middle line:84%
so if we say "Mary thinks
Susan must like herself," well,

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.520 align:middle line:90%
"herself" can refer to Susan.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:57.460 align:middle line:90%
But it can't refer to Mary.

00:19:57.460 --> 00:19:59.950 align:middle line:84%
So "Mary thinks Susan
must like herself."

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:02.173 align:middle line:84%
And that's not going
to be because "Mary"

00:20:02.173 --> 00:20:03.340 align:middle line:90%
doesn't c-command "herself."

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:05.740 align:middle line:84%
It does in the kinds of
trees that we're drawing.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:08.450 align:middle line:84%
So you are finding
a complication,

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:10.870 align:middle line:84%
which I was planning to
hide for a few more slides.

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:13.540 align:middle line:84%
There's an additional
requirement on anaphors.

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:16.673 align:middle line:84%
They don't just need to be
c-commanded by something

00:20:16.673 --> 00:20:17.590 align:middle line:90%
that corefers to them.

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:19.000 align:middle line:84%
They don't just
need to be bound.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:20.417 align:middle line:84%
They need to be
bound by something

00:20:20.417 --> 00:20:22.690 align:middle line:90%
that's sufficiently close.

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:24.308 align:middle line:84%
And if they're in
different clauses,

00:20:24.308 --> 00:20:25.600 align:middle line:90%
they're not sufficiently close.

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:26.980 align:middle line:90%
And we'll talk about that soon.

00:20:26.980 --> 00:20:28.750 align:middle line:84%
You'll get to look
back with nostalgia

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:32.920 align:middle line:84%
on this little interchange
in just a few minutes.

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:33.420 align:middle line:90%
Raquel?

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:35.045 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This isn't
really a question,

00:20:35.045 --> 00:20:37.190 align:middle line:84%
but I think it's
interesting that coreference

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:40.070 align:middle line:90%
could create a presupposition.

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:44.390 align:middle line:84%
"The magician made
herself disappear."

00:20:44.390 --> 00:20:46.040 align:middle line:84%
It's like, OK, well,
suddenly the fact

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:49.985 align:middle line:84%
that the magician is female is
a presupposition that's there.

00:20:49.985 --> 00:20:53.110 align:middle line:84%
But it could be wrong, and you
couldn't star it necessarily

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:54.110 align:middle line:90%
because you're not sure.

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:55.400 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:56.420 align:middle line:90%
No, I take your point.

00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:59.720 align:middle line:84%
So I take your point to be,
if you say "The magician made

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:02.240 align:middle line:84%
herself disappear,"
or "The magician made

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:04.490 align:middle line:84%
her father disappear,"
whether you

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:06.813 align:middle line:84%
use an anaphor or a
pronoun, "magician" doesn't

00:21:06.813 --> 00:21:08.480 align:middle line:84%
have any information
about gender in it.

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:11.420 align:middle line:84%
But a fact about-- a thing
about pronouns and reflexives

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:14.420 align:middle line:84%
in English is that they
carry presuppositions

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:15.920 align:middle line:84%
about the gender
of their reference.

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:16.970 align:middle line:90%
That's absolutely right.

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:17.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:19.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's quite true.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:20.742 align:middle line:90%
Yup.

00:21:20.742 --> 00:21:22.848 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:21:22.848 --> 00:21:23.890 align:middle line:90%
All right, so-- oh, good.

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:25.610 align:middle line:84%
I did say it on the
next slide, bind.

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:29.333 align:middle line:84%
This is just a name for
c-command and "corefer with"

00:21:29.333 --> 00:21:31.750 align:middle line:84%
just because we get tired of
saying c-command and "corefer

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:32.250 align:middle line:90%
with."

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:34.960 align:middle line:84%
We say alpha binds beta
if alpha c-commands

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:36.010 align:middle line:90%
and corefers with beta.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:40.810 align:middle line:84%
So in "Susan must like herself,"
"Susan" binds "herself."

00:21:40.810 --> 00:21:45.580 align:middle line:84%
"Susan" corefers with "herself,"
and it c-commands "herself."

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:47.590 align:middle line:90%
And anaphors need to be bound.

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:50.585 align:middle line:84%
And thanks to Faith, you
know that, pretty soon, it's

00:21:50.585 --> 00:21:52.210 align:middle line:84%
going to be more
complicated than that.

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:53.918 align:middle line:84%
They need to be bound
by something that's

00:21:53.918 --> 00:21:55.690 align:middle line:90%
sufficiently close to them.

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:57.870 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Speaking about
behavior of c-command,

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:04.116 align:middle line:84%
you mentioned earlier in the
semester how normal pronouns

00:22:04.116 --> 00:22:04.810 align:middle line:90%
or--

00:22:04.810 --> 00:22:07.383 align:middle line:84%
yeah, normal pronouns
are not A c-commanded.

00:22:07.383 --> 00:22:09.800 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so we're
going to do pronouns next.

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:10.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:10.640 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:11.630 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, that's right.

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:12.740 align:middle line:90%
We're going to get to that.

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:14.323 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I think
we're doing it next.

00:22:14.323 --> 00:22:15.620 align:middle line:90%
Anaphors must be bound.

00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:17.700 align:middle line:90%
OK, sorry, one more thing.

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:19.110 align:middle line:90%
There are two kinds of anaphors.

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:21.027 align:middle line:84%
There are reflexives,
which are the ones we've

00:22:21.027 --> 00:22:23.302 align:middle line:84%
been concentrating on, words
like herself and himself.

00:22:23.302 --> 00:22:25.260 align:middle line:84%
And then there are what
are called reciprocals,

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:26.468 align:middle line:90%
which have the same behavior.

00:22:26.468 --> 00:22:28.080 align:middle line:90%
They're words like "each other."

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:30.410 align:middle line:84%
So "John and Bill
like each other."

00:22:30.410 --> 00:22:34.100 align:middle line:84%
The meaning of "each other"
is much more complicated

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:36.020 align:middle line:84%
than the meaning
of "themselves,"

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:37.670 align:middle line:84%
but "each other"
and "themselves"

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:40.700 align:middle line:84%
have the same behavior in
that they are OK in sentences

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:43.130 align:middle line:84%
like "John and Bill like
each other" or "themselves,"

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:44.930 align:middle line:84%
and that they are
both bad in "John

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:47.570 align:middle line:84%
and Bill's father likes
each other" or "themselves."

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:50.870 align:middle line:84%
So "each other" and
"themselves" are both anaphors,

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:55.203 align:middle line:84%
and they're both subject to this
requirement that they be bound.

00:22:55.203 --> 00:22:56.870 align:middle line:84%
So "John and Bill
like each other" means

00:22:56.870 --> 00:23:00.110 align:middle line:84%
something like "John likes Bill
and Bill likes John," yeah?

00:23:00.110 --> 00:23:02.750 align:middle line:84%
There's literature on
exactly what reciprocals

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:05.730 align:middle line:90%
mean because it's complicated.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:07.910 align:middle line:84%
For example, it's possible
to say things like,

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:11.610 align:middle line:84%
"We piled the books
on top of each other."

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:15.260 align:middle line:84%
So each other doesn't
mean for every pair

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:19.803 align:middle line:84%
in the group A relation B
and B relation A, right?

00:23:19.803 --> 00:23:21.470 align:middle line:84%
You might have thought,
looking at this,

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:23.012 align:middle line:84%
that "John and Bill
like each other,"

00:23:23.012 --> 00:23:25.490 align:middle line:84%
to interpret that, what you do
is you take all of the pairs

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:26.420 align:middle line:90%
you can make out of the group.

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:27.920 align:middle line:90%
OK, there's one pair, you know?

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:31.700 align:middle line:84%
And what this means is John
likes Bill and Bill likes John.

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:34.620 align:middle line:84%
But if you say "The books are
piled on top of each other,"

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:38.930 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't mean this book
is on top of that book and--

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:40.215 align:middle line:90%
That's not what it means.

00:23:40.215 --> 00:23:41.840 align:middle line:84%
So there's work on
trying to figure out

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:44.150 align:middle line:84%
what the heck it
means, what reciprocals

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:49.640 align:middle line:84%
mean exactly, which we will
not get any further into now.

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:53.000 align:middle line:84%
OK, this was Joseph's
question, pronouns.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.480 align:middle line:90%
Anaphors must be bound.

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:55.633 align:middle line:90%
Pronouns must be free.

00:23:55.633 --> 00:23:57.050 align:middle line:84%
It sounds like a
political slogan,

00:23:57.050 --> 00:23:59.480 align:middle line:84%
but it's a fact
about grammar, yeah?

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:03.270 align:middle line:84%
So pronouns need
to not be bound.

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:05.570 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan likes
herself" is fine.

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:07.257 align:middle line:84%
"Susan's father likes
herself" is bad.

00:24:07.257 --> 00:24:08.840 align:middle line:84%
That's the behavior
of anaphors, which

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.550 align:middle line:90%
we're familiar with by now.

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:13.190 align:middle line:84%
And "her" behaves the
same way only backwards.

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:17.600 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan likes her," fine
sentence as long as "Susan"

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:19.070 align:middle line:90%
and "her" are different people.

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:21.823 align:middle line:84%
So "her" can't be
bound by "Susan."

00:24:21.823 --> 00:24:23.240 align:middle line:84%
It can't be
c-commanded by "Susan"

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:26.520 align:middle line:84%
if "Susan" and "her" are going
to refer to the same person.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:30.470 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, "Susan's
father likes her"-- fine.

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:34.770 align:middle line:84%
So it's not quite true,
but it's almost true

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:37.410 align:middle line:84%
that anaphors and pronouns are
in complementary distribution

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:38.330 align:middle line:90%
with each other.

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:41.340 align:middle line:84%
So for a given meaning,
you either use the pronoun,

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.980 align:middle line:90%
or you use the anaphor, OK?

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:47.445 align:middle line:90%


00:24:47.445 --> 00:24:47.945 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:47.945 --> 00:24:50.840 align:middle line:90%


00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:54.790 align:middle line:84%
More examples--
even more examples.

00:24:54.790 --> 00:24:58.560 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're getting
to Faith's point--

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:01.050 align:middle line:84%
my version of
Faith's point, sorry.

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:03.960 align:middle line:90%
So "Susan likes herself."

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:05.310 align:middle line:90%
We've seen this is OK.

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:06.870 align:middle line:90%
"Susan thinks I like herself."

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:09.840 align:middle line:84%
Or I have the
sentence on the board,

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:12.300 align:middle line:84%
"Mary thinks Susan
must like herself."

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:16.740 align:middle line:84%
"Herself" is trying to
get bound, in this case,

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:18.390 align:middle line:90%
on the slide by Susan.

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:20.670 align:middle line:90%
And it can't be.

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:22.260 align:middle line:84%
The kinds of trees
we were drawing

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:24.150 align:middle line:84%
for this kind of
sentence, Susan certainly

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:25.740 align:middle line:90%
does c-command herself.

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:29.660 align:middle line:84%
I don't think I put
the tree on the slide.

00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:31.890 align:middle line:84%
So let me just
create it quickly.

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:42.670 align:middle line:84%
"Susan thinks"-- there's going
to be a CP here, then null C.

00:25:42.670 --> 00:25:45.430 align:middle line:84%
And then down here,
we have a noun phrase,

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:52.990 align:middle line:84%
"I," and a verb
phrase like "herself."

00:25:52.990 --> 00:25:56.450 align:middle line:90%
So if we ask ourselves does--

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:57.640 align:middle line:90%
here's the tree.

00:25:57.640 --> 00:25:59.140 align:middle line:90%
Did I write the right sentence?

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:01.720 align:middle line:84%
"Susan thinks I
like herself," yes.

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:04.930 align:middle line:84%
If we ask ourself, does
"Susan" c-command "herself,"

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:06.520 align:middle line:90%
the answer is yes.

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:08.710 align:middle line:84%
If we ask what
are the nodes that

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:11.602 align:middle line:84%
dominate the noun
phrase "Susan," well,

00:26:11.602 --> 00:26:12.310 align:middle line:90%
there's only one.

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:14.200 align:middle line:90%
It's this TP.

00:26:14.200 --> 00:26:16.300 align:middle line:84%
And so if we're asking,
does "Susan" c-command

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:18.627 align:middle line:84%
"herself," well,
we're really asking,

00:26:18.627 --> 00:26:19.960 align:middle line:90%
does this TP dominate "herself"?

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:21.730 align:middle line:90%
And yes, it does.

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:25.800 align:middle line:90%
So "Susan" c-commands "herself."

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:29.100 align:middle line:84%
So it looks as though "Susan"
ought to bind "herself."

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:31.800 align:middle line:84%
What we're learning
here is the complication

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:35.865 align:middle line:84%
that Faith made me reveal
early, which is that not only do

00:26:35.865 --> 00:26:36.990 align:middle line:90%
anaphors need to get bound.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:38.070 align:middle line:84%
They need to get
bound by something

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:38.987 align:middle line:90%
which is close enough.

00:26:38.987 --> 00:26:42.000 align:middle line:90%


00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:43.560 align:middle line:90%
There's a principle.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:45.420 align:middle line:84%
It's called principle
A. You can think

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:47.190 align:middle line:90%
of A as standing for Anaphor.

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:52.320 align:middle line:84%
It says anaphors must
be bound within TP.

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:57.270 align:middle line:84%
If this were Intro to Syntax, we
would now spend weeks, possibly

00:26:57.270 --> 00:27:01.350 align:middle line:84%
months trying to figure
out what the real locality

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:03.720 align:middle line:84%
requirement is because it's
more complicated than that.

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:05.370 align:middle line:90%
But that's close.

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:10.680 align:middle line:84%
So the idea here is that
"herself," yes, it's

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:12.720 align:middle line:90%
bound by "Susan" in this tree.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:15.690 align:middle line:84%
But it's not bound by "Susan"
within the smallest TP.

00:27:15.690 --> 00:27:18.150 align:middle line:84%
Here's the smallest TP
that includes "herself,"

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:21.800 align:middle line:90%
and "Susan" is too far away.

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:25.715 align:middle line:84%
So there's a
locality requirement.

00:27:25.715 --> 00:27:26.590 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:28.510 align:middle line:84%
So principle A
says, anaphors need

00:27:28.510 --> 00:27:31.110 align:middle line:84%
to be bound within the
smallest TP that contains them.

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:33.920 align:middle line:90%


00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:37.430 align:middle line:84%
And then principle B, where B
stands for the principle that

00:27:37.430 --> 00:27:40.850 align:middle line:84%
comes after principle
A, says, pronouns

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:44.870 align:middle line:84%
must be free within TP,
so just as we saw before,

00:27:44.870 --> 00:27:46.700 align:middle line:84%
that pronouns and
anaphors are, so

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:49.520 align:middle line:84%
far, in complementary
distribution for a given

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:50.030 align:middle line:90%
reading.

00:27:50.030 --> 00:27:52.340 align:middle line:84%
So you can say "Susan
likes herself,"

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:55.340 align:middle line:84%
and that means that you
can't say "Susan likes her"

00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:56.930 align:middle line:84%
and mean the same
thing, that you mean

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:59.930 align:middle line:90%
that "Susan likes herself."

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:03.650 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, we've now seen,
thanks to principle A,

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:07.200 align:middle line:84%
that you cannot say "Susan
thinks I like herself,"

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:11.290 align:middle line:84%
where "herself" refers
back to "Susan."

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:14.110 align:middle line:84%
And principle B
says, yeah, pronouns

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:15.880 align:middle line:90%
have to be free within TP.

00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:18.430 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan thinks I like her,"
where "her" refers to "Susan."

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:20.640 align:middle line:84%
Fine, it doesn't have to
refer to "Susan," right?

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:21.640 align:middle line:90%
It can refer to anybody.

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:25.090 align:middle line:84%
It can be free regardless of
whether it's bound by "Susan"

00:28:25.090 --> 00:28:26.770 align:middle line:90%
or not, free within TP.

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:27.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Faith.

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:28.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I may
have just forgotten,

00:28:28.960 --> 00:28:33.250 align:middle line:84%
but with that T there, is the
invisible T that's dominated

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:35.080 align:middle line:90%
by T bar always necessary?

00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:36.400 align:middle line:90%
Or could you just say--

00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:36.910 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: This?

00:28:36.910 --> 00:28:37.535 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:28:37.535 --> 00:28:38.040 align:middle line:90%
And--

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:39.370 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: And also this?

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:39.870 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:40.460 align:middle line:90%
You know what?

00:28:40.460 --> 00:28:42.252 align:middle line:84%
In most of the trees
I've been showing you,

00:28:42.252 --> 00:28:45.305 align:middle line:84%
I've been smart enough to put
auxiliaries in the sentences.

00:28:45.305 --> 00:28:46.930 align:middle line:84%
So I've been saying
things like, "Susan

00:28:46.930 --> 00:28:50.650 align:middle line:84%
will think that I
must like herself."

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:53.980 align:middle line:84%
And then people don't ask the
question that you're asking.

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:57.400 align:middle line:84%
But when I drew this tree, I
was not smart enough to do that.

00:28:57.400 --> 00:29:01.933 align:middle line:84%
There does need to
be a T because--

00:29:01.933 --> 00:29:03.100 align:middle line:90%
why does there need to be T?

00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:06.850 align:middle line:84%
Well, we have principles
like the extended projection

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:10.330 align:middle line:84%
principle, which says that
TP must have a specifier.

00:29:10.330 --> 00:29:13.960 align:middle line:84%
And sentences without
auxiliaries in them

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:15.905 align:middle line:90%
show the effects of that.

00:29:15.905 --> 00:29:18.280 align:middle line:84%
So it doesn't matter whether
there's an auxiliary or not.

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:22.452 align:middle line:90%


00:29:22.452 --> 00:29:22.952 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:22.952 --> 00:29:24.770 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter whether
there's an auxiliary or not.

00:29:24.770 --> 00:29:27.395 align:middle line:84%
You still get the effects of the
extended projection principle.

00:29:27.395 --> 00:29:29.650 align:middle line:84%
So we do want there
to be a T because we

00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:30.598 align:middle line:90%
want there to be a TP.

00:29:30.598 --> 00:29:32.890 align:middle line:84%
And we don't have any way to
make a TP without starting

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:36.670 align:middle line:84%
with a T. So you have to merge
T with a verb phrase and so on.

00:29:36.670 --> 00:29:39.376 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:39.376 --> 00:29:40.533 align:middle line:90%


00:29:40.533 --> 00:29:41.450 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:42.500 align:middle line:90%
So I mean, here's--

00:29:42.500 --> 00:29:45.110 align:middle line:90%


00:29:45.110 --> 00:29:47.170 align:middle line:84%
I'm not totally unhappy
about the direction

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:48.920 align:middle line:84%
this conversation is
taking because you're

00:29:48.920 --> 00:29:52.940 align:middle line:84%
forcing me to talk about syntax,
which is fine, even though this

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:54.660 align:middle line:84%
is the semantic
part of the story.

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:58.380 align:middle line:84%
Suppose I was smart enough
to use an auxiliary here.

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:00.530 align:middle line:90%
So we'll do "Susan will think."

00:30:00.530 --> 00:30:03.535 align:middle line:84%
And we'll forget about the
embedded clause for a second.

00:30:03.535 --> 00:30:04.910 align:middle line:84%
We've talked about
this, I think,

00:30:04.910 --> 00:30:09.620 align:middle line:84%
that, if you want to
ask a yes/no question,

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:13.370 align:middle line:84%
the way to do it is
to head move T into C.

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:19.430 align:middle line:84%
So you ask questions like, "Will
Susan think that" blah-di-blah?

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:21.630 align:middle line:90%
So bear that in mind.

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:25.220 align:middle line:84%
And now, let's switch
to the version of this

00:30:25.220 --> 00:30:26.930 align:middle line:90%
where there isn't an auxiliary.

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:29.970 align:middle line:90%
"Susan thinks that I like."

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:32.570 align:middle line:84%
If I want to make a question
out of that, what I do

00:30:32.570 --> 00:30:35.000 align:middle line:90%
is I put "Does" here.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:36.630 align:middle line:90%
And I get rid of the S here.

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:40.400 align:middle line:84%
So it's "Does Susan
think that" blah-di-blah?

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:42.540 align:middle line:84%
So there's a story about
what's going on here,

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:45.740 align:middle line:84%
which says what's in T-- so
there are various things that

00:30:45.740 --> 00:30:50.840 align:middle line:84%
can be in T. There's "will"
and "should," "might."

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:56.750 align:middle line:84%
And there's also "-s," as in
"Mary thinks that I like her."

00:30:56.750 --> 00:31:01.382 align:middle line:84%
And if you do T to C of
"will" or "should" or "might,"

00:31:01.382 --> 00:31:02.840 align:middle line:84%
then you just move
them over there.

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:04.040 align:middle line:90%
And nothing else happens.

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:06.960 align:middle line:84%
If you have a "-s" then there
are a couple of things that

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:07.460 align:middle line:90%
happen.

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:10.430 align:middle line:84%
If you aren't head-moving it,
then it attaches to this verb.

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:11.920 align:middle line:84%
So you end up with
"Susan thinks."

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:14.420 align:middle line:84%
And we have to figure out how
these two things come together

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:15.380 align:middle line:90%
to form a verb.

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:20.360 align:middle line:84%
And sadly, it's long
enough since we did syntax

00:31:20.360 --> 00:31:23.000 align:middle line:84%
that I'm unwilling to
try to tell you that.

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.310 align:middle line:84%
There's a big literature on
trying to figure that out.

00:31:25.310 --> 00:31:27.902 align:middle line:84%
And then the other story is,
if you're going to do T to C,

00:31:27.902 --> 00:31:29.360 align:middle line:84%
if you're going to
make a question,

00:31:29.360 --> 00:31:30.470 align:middle line:90%
you're going to take this.

00:31:30.470 --> 00:31:32.090 align:middle line:90%
It's not going to show up here.

00:31:32.090 --> 00:31:33.960 align:middle line:90%
It's going to show up up here.

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:37.400 align:middle line:84%
So you would have expected this
to be "-s Susan think I like

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:38.060 align:middle line:90%
herself?"

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:41.050 align:middle line:84%
But "-s" is too small to be an
English word or something like

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:41.550 align:middle line:90%
that.

00:31:41.550 --> 00:31:45.030 align:middle line:90%
And so you put in this "do."

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:48.110 align:middle line:84%
There's a process that
is called "do support."

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:51.480 align:middle line:84%
You add "do" there so that
we'll have a verb to be part of.

00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:53.300 align:middle line:90%
It's the story that people tell.

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:55.550 align:middle line:84%
And so that's why the
question version of that

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:58.100 align:middle line:84%
is "Does Susan think
that I like her?"

00:31:58.100 --> 00:32:01.220 align:middle line:84%
Can you see why I
have been hiding all

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:03.200 align:middle line:90%
of this from you all semester?

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.660 align:middle line:84%
So I've been using auxiliaries
instead of giving you verbs

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:07.100 align:middle line:90%
that have suffixes on them.

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:09.898 align:middle line:84%
This is why, because I
didn't want to do this.

00:32:09.898 --> 00:32:10.940 align:middle line:90%
But it's been fun anyway.

00:32:10.940 --> 00:32:12.440 align:middle line:84%
But now, it's over,
except it's not.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:14.325 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:32:14.325 --> 00:32:15.700 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just a
brief question--

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:15.827 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:32:15.827 --> 00:32:17.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --about
that movement, you

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:19.240 align:middle line:84%
said the extended production
principle requires

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:21.040 align:middle line:90%
you TP has a specifier.

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:24.960 align:middle line:84%
So that means that, because
[INAUDIBLE] creating

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:28.430 align:middle line:84%
a yes or no question means
moving ahead out of TP,

00:32:28.430 --> 00:32:30.400 align:middle line:90%
you can't move "Susan."

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:32.453 align:middle line:84%
Because then TP was
the specifier, right?

00:32:32.453 --> 00:32:33.370 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:34.180 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:32:34.180 --> 00:32:34.900 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, why [INAUDIBLE]?

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:35.800 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No, no.

00:32:35.800 --> 00:32:37.508 align:middle line:84%
Oh, you mean why are
you moving the head?

00:32:37.508 --> 00:32:39.580 align:middle line:90%
Why are you not moving a phrase?

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:41.747 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why can't you
just move the phrase around?

00:32:41.747 --> 00:32:43.540 align:middle line:90%


00:32:43.540 --> 00:32:46.120 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: There isn't a
deep answer to that question.

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.730 align:middle line:84%
Forming questions involves
taking whatever is in T

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:51.550 align:middle line:84%
and moving it into
C. You'd like to know

00:32:51.550 --> 00:32:56.100 align:middle line:84%
why we don't move other things,
and that's a good question.

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:56.730 align:middle line:90%
This is--

00:32:56.730 --> 00:32:57.696 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] to her.

00:32:57.696 --> 00:33:00.070 align:middle line:84%
And I said, you can't do this
because of the shortest path.

00:33:00.070 --> 00:33:00.580 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah, yes.

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:00.880 align:middle line:90%
I see.

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:01.180 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:33:01.180 --> 00:33:03.650 align:middle line:84%
So, yeah, you can't take the
verb and move it up there.

00:33:03.650 --> 00:33:05.192 align:middle line:84%
That's right because
that's too long.

00:33:05.192 --> 00:33:07.540 align:middle line:90%
Why can't you move this?

00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:09.040 align:middle line:90%
Because it's not a head.

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:10.960 align:middle line:90%
It's a phrase.

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:13.060 align:middle line:84%
Apparently, we think
that only heads can

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:14.480 align:middle line:90%
move to attach to other heads.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:14.980 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:16.692 align:middle line:90%
And we might want to know why.

00:33:16.692 --> 00:33:18.650 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of things
we might want to know.

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:20.080 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:20.650 align:middle line:90%


00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:22.270 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Go take syntax.

00:33:22.270 --> 00:33:24.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:33:24.310 --> 00:33:25.780 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:26.830 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:29.650 align:middle line:84%
All right, all of
you, you dragged

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:31.990 align:middle line:84%
me kicking and screaming
into doing more syntax.

00:33:31.990 --> 00:33:32.560 align:middle line:90%
Shame on you.

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:35.350 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:35.350 --> 00:33:37.090 align:middle line:84%
Right, principle A,
principle B, yes?

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:39.940 align:middle line:84%
Are people reasonably happy with
principle A and principle B?

00:33:39.940 --> 00:33:42.220 align:middle line:84%
So they say anaphors need
to be bound within TP.

00:33:42.220 --> 00:33:43.690 align:middle line:84%
Pronouns have to
be free within TP.

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:46.800 align:middle line:90%


00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:50.520 align:middle line:84%
Side note, since I just told
you universal about anaphors,

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:52.290 align:middle line:84%
anaphors only ever
care about c-command.

00:33:52.290 --> 00:33:54.715 align:middle line:84%
They never seem to care
about linear order.

00:33:54.715 --> 00:33:57.090 align:middle line:84%
They don't care about what
precedes them or follows them.

00:33:57.090 --> 00:33:59.220 align:middle line:84%
I should say that
there are points

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:00.780 align:middle line:90%
of variation between languages.

00:34:00.780 --> 00:34:04.620 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that have
anaphors that need to be bound,

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:07.620 align:middle line:84%
but they can be bound
longer than this.

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:09.330 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
that have anaphors

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:10.980 align:middle line:84%
that can be bound
outside of the TP

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:13.270 align:middle line:84%
that they're in called
long-distance anaphors.

00:34:13.270 --> 00:34:16.739 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't have them, but
Mandarin does, for example.

00:34:16.739 --> 00:34:18.989 align:middle line:84%
So Mandarin, [NON-ENGLISH]
is a long-distance anaphor.

00:34:18.989 --> 00:34:22.739 align:middle line:90%


00:34:22.739 --> 00:34:25.500 align:middle line:84%
Principle A is not a parameter
that distinguishes languages.

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:27.420 align:middle line:84%
It's a parameter that
distinguishes anaphors.

00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:29.722 align:middle line:84%
So Mandarin actually
has two anaphors.

00:34:29.722 --> 00:34:31.430 align:middle line:84%
It has an anaphor that
behaves like this.

00:34:31.430 --> 00:34:33.600 align:middle line:84%
It's just pronounced
[NON-ENGLISH]..

00:34:33.600 --> 00:34:37.170 align:middle line:84%
And then it has another one
that is long distance that can

00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:38.610 align:middle line:90%
be bound from outside its TP.

00:34:38.610 --> 00:34:41.040 align:middle line:84%
It's just [NON-ENGLISH]
by itself.

00:34:41.040 --> 00:34:44.310 align:middle line:84%
Those of you who speak Mandarin
can be thinking about that.

00:34:44.310 --> 00:34:47.040 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that
have more anaphors than that.

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:48.659 align:middle line:84%
Korean, I think,
holds the record.

00:34:48.659 --> 00:34:50.909 align:middle line:84%
It has four different anaphors
with slightly different

00:34:50.909 --> 00:34:51.690 align:middle line:90%
behaviors.

00:34:51.690 --> 00:34:52.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:54.600 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do languages that
have long-distance anaphors

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:59.490 align:middle line:84%
also always have this type
of anaphor, or does it vary?

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:03.120 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
As far as I know,

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:05.970 align:middle line:84%
every language has
this type of anaphor.

00:35:05.970 --> 00:35:12.370 align:middle line:84%
And long-distance anaphors
are an additional luxury

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:14.222 align:middle line:84%
that a language can
allow itself or not.

00:35:14.222 --> 00:35:16.180 align:middle line:84%
I don't think there are
any languages that only

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:17.830 align:middle line:90%
have long-distance anaphors.

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:20.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good question.

00:35:20.570 --> 00:35:21.070 align:middle line:90%
Yup?

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:21.570 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:35:21.570 --> 00:35:27.670 align:middle line:90%


00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:28.270 align:middle line:90%
Where are we?

00:35:28.270 --> 00:35:29.020 align:middle line:90%
OK, yes.

00:35:29.020 --> 00:35:32.980 align:middle line:84%
So we have principle A. We
have principle B. Anaphors need

00:35:32.980 --> 00:35:33.940 align:middle line:90%
to be bound within TP.

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:35.500 align:middle line:84%
Pronouns have to
be free within TP.

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:38.020 align:middle line:84%
And let's stick
to English again.

00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:40.390 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's consider these
sentences, "She likes Susan"

00:35:40.390 --> 00:35:47.612 align:middle line:84%
and "Her father likes
Susan," with these subscripts

00:35:47.612 --> 00:35:49.320 align:middle line:84%
and these grammaticality
judgments, which

00:35:49.320 --> 00:35:50.530 align:middle line:90%
I hope everybody agrees with.

00:35:50.530 --> 00:35:52.018 align:middle line:90%
So "She likes Susan"--

00:35:52.018 --> 00:35:54.435 align:middle line:84%
fine sentence, but she and
Susan can't be the same person.

00:35:54.435 --> 00:35:56.803 align:middle line:90%


00:35:56.803 --> 00:35:58.845 align:middle line:84%
Does either of our principles
handle these facts?

00:35:58.845 --> 00:36:03.380 align:middle line:90%


00:36:03.380 --> 00:36:07.910 align:middle line:84%
That's a leading question
if there ever was one.

00:36:07.910 --> 00:36:09.750 align:middle line:90%
No, they do not.

00:36:09.750 --> 00:36:11.938 align:middle line:84%
So first of all,
principle A is obviously

00:36:11.938 --> 00:36:12.980 align:middle line:90%
going to be useless here.

00:36:12.980 --> 00:36:15.560 align:middle line:84%
There are no anaphors
in these sentences.

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:17.630 align:middle line:84%
So forget principle
A. Principle B,

00:36:17.630 --> 00:36:20.880 align:middle line:84%
pronouns need to
be free within TP.

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:24.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, is she free within
TP in "She likes Susan"?

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:30.040 align:middle line:84%
Is there a noun phrase that
c-commands "she" and corefers

00:36:30.040 --> 00:36:30.540 align:middle line:90%
with it?

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:34.990 align:middle line:90%


00:36:34.990 --> 00:36:37.220 align:middle line:90%
Is "she" free?

00:36:37.220 --> 00:36:38.450 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:39.260 align:middle line:90%
She is free.

00:36:39.260 --> 00:36:40.310 align:middle line:90%
Shall I draw a tree?

00:36:40.310 --> 00:36:43.400 align:middle line:90%


00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:44.570 align:middle line:90%
What's the sentence?

00:36:44.570 --> 00:36:45.320 align:middle line:90%
"She likes Susan."

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:50.350 align:middle line:90%


00:36:50.350 --> 00:36:54.700 align:middle line:84%
Someday, I'm going
to ask somebody

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:58.090 align:middle line:84%
to make me a stamp, a
chalk stamp, you know,

00:36:58.090 --> 00:37:01.040 align:middle line:84%
that just has this tree,
TP, T bar, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:01.540 align:middle line:90%
Wham.

00:37:01.540 --> 00:37:03.000 align:middle line:90%
And then there it would be.

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:03.860 align:middle line:90%
That would be great.

00:37:03.860 --> 00:37:05.950 align:middle line:90%
"She likes Susan," yeah?

00:37:05.950 --> 00:37:06.910 align:middle line:90%
Here's the pronoun.

00:37:06.910 --> 00:37:08.560 align:middle line:84%
Pronoun needs to
be free within TP.

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:11.410 align:middle line:84%
That is there had better not
be anything c-commanding "she"

00:37:11.410 --> 00:37:12.580 align:middle line:90%
that corefers with "she."

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:13.990 align:middle line:90%
Does anything c-command "she"?

00:37:13.990 --> 00:37:19.420 align:middle line:90%


00:37:19.420 --> 00:37:23.110 align:middle line:90%
I guess maybe T bar.

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:27.670 align:middle line:84%
So T bar is dominated by all
the same nodes as noun phrase.

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:29.263 align:middle line:84%
But T bar doesn't
refer to "she."

00:37:29.263 --> 00:37:30.430 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't refer to "Susan."

00:37:30.430 --> 00:37:31.870 align:middle line:90%
So "she" is free, yeah.

00:37:31.870 --> 00:37:35.700 align:middle line:84%
So our principles don't
handle these facts.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:38.310 align:middle line:84%
There are at least two
things we could do.

00:37:38.310 --> 00:37:40.740 align:middle line:84%
One would be to say,
ah, principle B,

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:46.650 align:middle line:84%
we were wrong to confine
principle B to pronouns.

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:50.790 align:middle line:84%
So it's really just a
distinction between anaphors

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:52.750 align:middle line:90%
and everything else.

00:37:52.750 --> 00:37:56.070 align:middle line:84%
So the problem with "She
likes Susan" isn't "she."

00:37:56.070 --> 00:37:57.750 align:middle line:90%
It's "Susan."

00:37:57.750 --> 00:38:00.870 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan" also needs
to be free within TP.

00:38:00.870 --> 00:38:03.150 align:middle line:84%
And then the story would be
"Her father likes Susan."

00:38:03.150 --> 00:38:05.530 align:middle line:84%
"Her" is embedded
inside "her father."

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:07.840 align:middle line:84%
And so it doesn't c-command
"Susan," and so there.

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:12.170 align:middle line:84%
That version of principle
B would cover this.

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:21.920 align:middle line:84%
But pronouns really are
the opposite of anaphors.

00:38:21.920 --> 00:38:23.690 align:middle line:90%
They need to be free within TP.

00:38:23.690 --> 00:38:27.650 align:middle line:84%
So "Susan thinks I like her,"
where "Susan" c-commands "her"

00:38:27.650 --> 00:38:30.638 align:middle line:84%
and corefers with "her," so
"Susan" binds "her," is fine.

00:38:30.638 --> 00:38:32.180 align:middle line:84%
Because although
"her" is bound, it's

00:38:32.180 --> 00:38:34.290 align:middle line:84%
bound by something
that's far away.

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:36.180 align:middle line:90%
And that's all right.

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:38.800 align:middle line:84%
But in "She thinks
I like Susan,"

00:38:38.800 --> 00:38:42.480 align:middle line:84%
we're seeing that names do
not behave like pronouns.

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:47.940 align:middle line:84%
"Susan" requires absolute
freedom, freedom everywhere.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:51.060 align:middle line:84%
Pronouns just need to be
free within a certain domain,

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:52.500 align:middle line:90%
within TP let's say.

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:56.640 align:middle line:84%
But a name like "Susan"
needs to be free everywhere.

00:38:56.640 --> 00:38:58.650 align:middle line:84%
The fact that "Susan"
is bound by "she"

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:00.900 align:middle line:84%
in that second example,
"She thinks I like Susan,"

00:39:00.900 --> 00:39:02.207 align:middle line:90%
means that the sentence is bad.

00:39:02.207 --> 00:39:03.915 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter that
they're far apart.

00:39:03.915 --> 00:39:07.990 align:middle line:90%


00:39:07.990 --> 00:39:11.440 align:middle line:84%
So we make up a new principle--
it's called principle C--

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:14.410 align:middle line:84%
and, also, a new
term, R-expressions.

00:39:14.410 --> 00:39:19.720 align:middle line:84%
R-expressions means things that
are not anaphors or pronouns.

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:22.940 align:middle line:84%
It stands for
Referring expressions,

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:26.350 align:middle line:84%
which is an unfortunate name
because they don't necessarily

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:27.248 align:middle line:90%
refer.

00:39:27.248 --> 00:39:27.790 align:middle line:90%
"Susan" does.

00:39:27.790 --> 00:39:28.582 align:middle line:90%
It refers to Susan.

00:39:28.582 --> 00:39:31.600 align:middle line:90%
But anyway, that's all it means.

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:33.340 align:middle line:84%
Things that are not
anaphors or pronouns.

00:39:33.340 --> 00:39:36.910 align:middle line:84%
So everything that isn't subject
to principle A or principle B

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:39.670 align:middle line:84%
is subject to principle
C. Every noun phrase that

00:39:39.670 --> 00:39:41.650 align:middle line:84%
isn't subject to
principle A or principal B

00:39:41.650 --> 00:39:44.590 align:middle line:84%
is subject to principle C,
which says our expressions must

00:39:44.590 --> 00:39:47.223 align:middle line:90%
be free everywhere.

00:39:47.223 --> 00:39:49.390 align:middle line:84%
Our expressions must be
free, and everywhere they're

00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:50.540 align:middle line:90%
in chains.

00:39:50.540 --> 00:39:53.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, OK?

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:54.430 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:39:54.430 --> 00:39:58.390 align:middle line:84%
And then this is the fact that
I made a big deal about before.

00:39:58.390 --> 00:40:02.860 align:middle line:84%
Even though which thing
precedes which other thing is

00:40:02.860 --> 00:40:05.470 align:middle line:84%
the kind of thing that every
infant knows as soon as they

00:40:05.470 --> 00:40:08.410 align:middle line:84%
can hear, words and segment
words, which infants

00:40:08.410 --> 00:40:13.300 align:middle line:84%
are eerily good
at, even though--

00:40:13.300 --> 00:40:19.090 align:middle line:84%
if you were going to design
a computer, a language

00:40:19.090 --> 00:40:21.880 align:middle line:90%
for computers to use--

00:40:21.880 --> 00:40:23.380 align:middle line:90%
I am not a computer person.

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:25.030 align:middle line:90%
You may have picked up on this.

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:28.240 align:middle line:84%
Even I think it would be
easier for me to get a computer

00:40:28.240 --> 00:40:31.960 align:middle line:84%
to figure out which thing
linearly precedes which

00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:34.240 align:middle line:84%
other thing than it would
be to get a computer

00:40:34.240 --> 00:40:36.910 align:middle line:84%
to figure out which thing
c-commands which other things.

00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:38.603 align:middle line:84%
That's a much more
complicated task,

00:40:38.603 --> 00:40:41.020 align:middle line:84%
it seems to me, not that I
know anything about this stuff.

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:43.840 align:middle line:84%
And yet all of us go for
the more complicated task.

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:44.840 align:middle line:90%
We're built that way.

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:45.850 align:middle line:90%
We can't help it.

00:40:45.850 --> 00:40:47.800 align:middle line:84%
So language doesn't
care which thing

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:49.000 align:middle line:90%
precedes which other thing.

00:40:49.000 --> 00:40:51.520 align:middle line:84%
It cares which things c-commands
which other thing, always,

00:40:51.520 --> 00:40:53.740 align:middle line:84%
in every language,
over and over again.

00:40:53.740 --> 00:40:54.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:40:54.490 --> 00:40:58.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do we draw a tree
when the subordinate phrase is

00:40:58.490 --> 00:40:59.127 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:59.127 --> 00:40:59.960 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh.

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:02.540 align:middle line:84%
Well, we could do
different ways.

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:06.710 align:middle line:90%


00:41:06.710 --> 00:41:08.510 align:middle line:90%
Where will I put this?

00:41:08.510 --> 00:41:09.350 align:middle line:90%
I'll put it up here.

00:41:09.350 --> 00:41:13.320 align:middle line:90%


00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So maybe you would start
by drawing the TP first.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:33.970 align:middle line:90%
So "Susan read a book," right?

00:41:33.970 --> 00:41:36.220 align:middle line:90%
Do that for "Susan read a book."

00:41:36.220 --> 00:41:40.480 align:middle line:84%
And then we need somewhere to
hang "while she was eating."

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:43.780 align:middle line:84%
And we need it to be
some kind of adjunct.

00:41:43.780 --> 00:41:50.400 align:middle line:84%
So it's not in the same kind
of relation as anything else.

00:41:50.400 --> 00:41:51.400 align:middle line:90%
What am I trying to say?

00:41:51.400 --> 00:41:52.780 align:middle line:90%
It's not selected by anything.

00:41:52.780 --> 00:41:55.490 align:middle line:84%
There's no particular reason
for it to be anywhere.

00:41:55.490 --> 00:41:59.140 align:middle line:84%
So there are various
ways we could do that.

00:41:59.140 --> 00:42:02.980 align:middle line:84%
Notice, for example, that if
this were an embedded clause,

00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:07.850 align:middle line:90%
so if it were "I think that"--

00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:12.556 align:middle line:84%
so here's a C. It's
going to be a CP--

00:42:12.556 --> 00:42:15.730 align:middle line:90%
sorry, a CP.

00:42:15.730 --> 00:42:18.580 align:middle line:84%
So if we were doing, "I think
that Susan read a book,"

00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:20.563 align:middle line:84%
the embedded clause
"that Susan read a book"

00:42:20.563 --> 00:42:21.730 align:middle line:90%
would look like this, right?

00:42:21.730 --> 00:42:24.430 align:middle line:84%
It would have a CP here where
I can just connect these.

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:27.040 align:middle line:84%
We have a CP with
a C that that's

00:42:27.040 --> 00:42:29.800 align:middle line:90%
got this TP as its sister.

00:42:29.800 --> 00:42:32.920 align:middle line:84%
And if we were going to
add this embedded clause,

00:42:32.920 --> 00:42:35.410 align:middle line:84%
this adjuncty clause
to the beginning

00:42:35.410 --> 00:42:40.360 align:middle line:84%
of the embedded clause,
while she was eating,

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:41.830 align:middle line:84%
let's think about
places to put it.

00:42:41.830 --> 00:42:44.740 align:middle line:84%
If it's going to be before
"Susan," we could ask,

00:42:44.740 --> 00:42:45.940 align:middle line:90%
does it go here?

00:42:45.940 --> 00:42:47.710 align:middle line:90%
Or does it go here?

00:42:47.710 --> 00:42:50.770 align:middle line:84%
So "I think that,
while she was eating,

00:42:50.770 --> 00:42:55.290 align:middle line:84%
Susan read a book" is, I think,
something that we can say.

00:42:55.290 --> 00:42:57.855 align:middle line:84%
"I think, while she was eating,
that Susan read a book."

00:42:57.855 --> 00:43:02.520 align:middle line:90%


00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:04.620 align:middle line:84%
I think does not
mean the same thing.

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:06.820 align:middle line:84%
There's something
wrong with that.

00:43:06.820 --> 00:43:08.850 align:middle line:84%
So this is at least
one place that it can

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:13.097 align:middle line:90%
go between the C and the TP.

00:43:13.097 --> 00:43:15.180 align:middle line:84%
And given that it can go
between the C and the TP,

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:17.370 align:middle line:84%
maybe the easiest thing
to do would be to just

00:43:17.370 --> 00:43:20.550 align:middle line:90%
make it an adjunct on TP.

00:43:20.550 --> 00:43:22.980 align:middle line:90%
We'll duplicate the TP node.

00:43:22.980 --> 00:43:27.030 align:middle line:84%
And we'll have a CP here where
I won't do the insides of it,

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:29.395 align:middle line:84%
but that's where you would
put "while she was eating."

00:43:29.395 --> 00:43:30.270 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:43:30.270 --> 00:43:32.830 align:middle line:90%


00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:34.235 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:43:34.235 --> 00:43:34.735 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:43:34.735 --> 00:43:38.060 align:middle line:90%


00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:39.710 align:middle line:90%
So that's binding theory.

00:43:39.710 --> 00:43:41.960 align:middle line:84%
I think I flagged the fact
there are many, many things

00:43:41.960 --> 00:43:43.127 align:middle line:90%
to say about binding theory.

00:43:43.127 --> 00:43:44.630 align:middle line:90%
That's part of it.

00:43:44.630 --> 00:43:47.780 align:middle line:84%
Reasons to be happy
about binding theory,

00:43:47.780 --> 00:43:50.060 align:middle line:84%
I first showed you
binding theory as a probe

00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:52.278 align:middle line:84%
into c-command relations,
but there's so much more.

00:43:52.278 --> 00:43:54.570 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of things we
can do with binding theory.

00:43:54.570 --> 00:43:56.300 align:middle line:90%
Let me show you one.

00:43:56.300 --> 00:43:57.290 align:middle line:90%
Here's a sentence.

00:43:57.290 --> 00:43:59.270 align:middle line:90%
"Mary decided to leave."

00:43:59.270 --> 00:44:00.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, fine sentence.

00:44:00.590 --> 00:44:03.400 align:middle line:90%


00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:05.515 align:middle line:84%
How many TPs are
in this sentence?

00:44:05.515 --> 00:44:09.360 align:middle line:90%


00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:12.720 align:middle line:90%
well there's at least one.

00:44:12.720 --> 00:44:14.265 align:middle line:90%
What's "to leave"?

00:44:14.265 --> 00:44:19.290 align:middle line:90%


00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:21.900 align:middle line:90%
"To" isn't a preposition here.

00:44:21.900 --> 00:44:27.090 align:middle line:84%
This isn't like "Mary
went to Toledo," yeah?

00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:39.920 align:middle line:84%
In fact, this "to" is in
complementary distribution

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:44.300 align:middle line:84%
with a bunch of
different things.

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:46.070 align:middle line:90%
You can't say, for example--

00:44:46.070 --> 00:44:47.570 align:middle line:84%
you can say "Mary
decided to leave."

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:49.910 align:middle line:84%
You can't say "Mary
decided will leave"

00:44:49.910 --> 00:44:57.650 align:middle line:84%
or "should leave" or, for that
matter, "Mary decided left."

00:44:57.650 --> 00:44:59.480 align:middle line:90%
These are not sentences.

00:44:59.480 --> 00:45:05.720 align:middle line:84%
So these are all bad, which
makes people think that maybe

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:09.860 align:middle line:84%
"to" is the same kind of
thing as "will" and "should."

00:45:09.860 --> 00:45:12.443 align:middle line:84%
So here's a spot where you can
only have one of these things.

00:45:12.443 --> 00:45:14.360 align:middle line:84%
You can have "will," or
you can have "should,"

00:45:14.360 --> 00:45:17.170 align:middle line:90%
or you can "have to."

00:45:17.170 --> 00:45:19.180 align:middle line:90%
And so people think-- yeah.

00:45:19.180 --> 00:45:21.730 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is "to" not
joined with the verb

00:45:21.730 --> 00:45:24.910 align:middle line:84%
itself, though, as
part of the infinitive?

00:45:24.910 --> 00:45:26.295 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I don't know.

00:45:26.295 --> 00:45:28.420 align:middle line:84%
This gets into questions
about whether you believed

00:45:28.420 --> 00:45:33.040 align:middle line:84%
your English teacher when she or
he told you that you cannot say

00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:39.070 align:middle line:84%
things like "Mary decided
to immediately leave,

00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:41.935 align:middle line:84%
but we decided to boldly go
where no one has gone before."

00:45:41.935 --> 00:45:44.800 align:middle line:90%


00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:50.070 align:middle line:84%
There are languages in which
the marker of the infinitive

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:51.820 align:middle line:84%
is a morpheme that's
attached to the verb.

00:45:51.820 --> 00:45:53.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you're absolutely right.

00:45:53.230 --> 00:45:55.390 align:middle line:84%
But in English, it's
not so clear that it is.

00:45:55.390 --> 00:45:58.240 align:middle line:84%
There's stuff like adverbs that
you can put between the "to"

00:45:58.240 --> 00:46:01.760 align:middle line:90%
and the verb, at least I can.

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:03.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:46:03.120 --> 00:46:04.090 align:middle line:90%
All right, Joseph?

00:46:04.090 --> 00:46:07.370 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is the "to" shorthand
for "that she should," "would,"

00:46:07.370 --> 00:46:07.870 align:middle line:90%
or--

00:46:07.870 --> 00:46:09.840 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Well, so this sentence

00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:11.590 align:middle line:84%
means more or less the
same thing as "Mary

00:46:11.590 --> 00:46:12.970 align:middle line:90%
decided that she would leave."

00:46:12.970 --> 00:46:20.710 align:middle line:90%


00:46:20.710 --> 00:46:23.278 align:middle line:84%
So interesting fact,
you can't have "that"

00:46:23.278 --> 00:46:24.570 align:middle line:90%
if you're going to have a "to."

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:27.920 align:middle line:90%


00:46:27.920 --> 00:46:28.420 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:28.420 --> 00:46:30.100 align:middle line:84%
If you have a "that," then
you cannot have a "to."

00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:32.920 align:middle line:84%
If you have "that," then you
can have all these other things

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:35.080 align:middle line:84%
that would be here if
"to" were not here.

00:46:35.080 --> 00:46:36.730 align:middle line:84%
You could have a
"will," or a "should,"

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:39.140 align:middle line:84%
or a past tense, or all
of these other things.

00:46:39.140 --> 00:46:40.630 align:middle line:84%
That's the relation
that we talked

00:46:40.630 --> 00:46:41.797 align:middle line:90%
about in terms of selection.

00:46:41.797 --> 00:46:46.660 align:middle line:84%
We said C selects T.
T is selected by C.

00:46:46.660 --> 00:46:52.180 align:middle line:84%
So these are all reasons to
think maybe that "to" is a T.

00:46:52.180 --> 00:46:54.040 align:middle line:84%
There are two TPs
in this sentence.

00:46:54.040 --> 00:46:56.560 align:middle line:90%
There's "Mary decided to leave."

00:46:56.560 --> 00:46:57.250 align:middle line:90%
That's one TP.

00:46:57.250 --> 00:47:02.410 align:middle line:84%
And that "to leave"
is another TP, OK?

00:47:02.410 --> 00:47:04.090 align:middle line:90%
Are you buying this?

00:47:04.090 --> 00:47:04.930 align:middle line:90%
You should.

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:07.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:47:07.080 --> 00:47:09.000 align:middle line:90%
OK, so maybe there are two TPs.

00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:11.713 align:middle line:84%
But if there are two
TPs in this sentence,

00:47:11.713 --> 00:47:14.130 align:middle line:84%
we have this principle, the
extended projection principle,

00:47:14.130 --> 00:47:17.460 align:middle line:84%
that says every TP
must have a specifier.

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:22.230 align:middle line:84%
But we only seem to have
one subject, "Mary,"

00:47:22.230 --> 00:47:25.240 align:middle line:90%
who's doing the deciding.

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:29.080 align:middle line:84%
Now, there's a classic answer
to this question, which

00:47:29.080 --> 00:47:35.350 align:middle line:84%
says English is a language with
many pronouns, and one of them

00:47:35.350 --> 00:47:38.050 align:middle line:90%
is invisible.

00:47:38.050 --> 00:47:41.770 align:middle line:84%
And the invisible one is the one
that is the subject of "leave."

00:47:41.770 --> 00:47:44.260 align:middle line:84%
It's the thing that's
satisfying the EPP here.

00:47:44.260 --> 00:47:48.400 align:middle line:84%
So it isn't that TP doesn't
have a specifier in "to leave."

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:49.960 align:middle line:90%
That TP does have a specifier.

00:47:49.960 --> 00:47:54.070 align:middle line:84%
It's just an
invisible specifier.

00:47:54.070 --> 00:47:59.480 align:middle line:84%
When this theory
was first offered,

00:47:59.480 --> 00:48:02.740 align:middle line:84%
people reacted the way
you might expect them to,

00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:04.850 align:middle line:84%
sort of the way some of
you possibly mentally are:

00:48:04.850 --> 00:48:08.320 align:middle line:84%
"I mean, come on,
you're just trying

00:48:08.320 --> 00:48:10.750 align:middle line:84%
to avoid having to say
that the EPP is not right

00:48:10.750 --> 00:48:14.560 align:middle line:84%
or parameterize the EPP to
different kinds of positions."

00:48:14.560 --> 00:48:16.120 align:middle line:84%
But actually, binding
theory gives us

00:48:16.120 --> 00:48:19.270 align:middle line:84%
some reasons to believe that
there is such a thing as PRO.

00:48:19.270 --> 00:48:22.040 align:middle line:84%
And let me show you
some of the arguments.

00:48:22.040 --> 00:48:24.670 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, that's
a story that says,

00:48:24.670 --> 00:48:27.010 align:middle line:84%
no, the EPP is being satisfied
in the embedded clause

00:48:27.010 --> 00:48:30.085 align:middle line:84%
by an invisible pronoun that
refers, in this case, to Mary.

00:48:30.085 --> 00:48:33.120 align:middle line:90%


00:48:33.120 --> 00:48:36.000 align:middle line:90%
Here are some data.

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:37.920 align:middle line:84%
"John promised Mary
to defend himself."

00:48:37.920 --> 00:48:39.900 align:middle line:84%
"John promised Mary
to defend herself."

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:41.670 align:middle line:84%
"John told Mary to
defend himself."

00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:44.310 align:middle line:84%
"John told Mary to
defend herself"--

00:48:44.310 --> 00:48:47.910 align:middle line:84%
sentences with
grammaticality judgments.

00:48:47.910 --> 00:48:50.110 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to copy these
sentences onto the board

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:52.360 align:middle line:84%
because I want to do various
graphic things with them.

00:48:52.360 --> 00:48:54.450 align:middle line:84%
And while I'm copying
them, I'll ask

00:48:54.450 --> 00:48:58.740 align:middle line:84%
you to look at those sentences
and ask yourself whether you

00:48:58.740 --> 00:49:00.300 align:middle line:90%
get these judgments.

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:11.590 align:middle line:84%
So "John promised Mary
to defend herself"--

00:49:11.590 --> 00:49:13.902 align:middle line:90%
"himself."

00:49:13.902 --> 00:49:15.360 align:middle line:84%
Did I put those in
the wrong order?

00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:16.050 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I did.

00:49:16.050 --> 00:49:19.620 align:middle line:90%


00:49:19.620 --> 00:49:33.492 align:middle line:84%
And then "John told Mary
to defend himself"--

00:49:33.492 --> 00:49:40.110 align:middle line:84%
"herself," where the
middle two-- so this is bad

00:49:40.110 --> 00:49:41.390 align:middle line:90%
and this is bad.

00:49:41.390 --> 00:49:42.640 align:middle line:90%
Do people get these judgments?

00:49:42.640 --> 00:49:43.420 align:middle line:90%
Is this true?

00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:45.050 align:middle line:90%
Am I just making this up?

00:49:45.050 --> 00:49:46.150 align:middle line:90%
Nobody wants to protest?

00:49:46.150 --> 00:49:46.690 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:49:46.690 --> 00:49:51.250 align:middle line:84%
So I told you before we're
going to use binding theory

00:49:51.250 --> 00:49:56.353 align:middle line:84%
to find things out about how far
away things are, which things

00:49:56.353 --> 00:49:58.270 align:middle line:84%
c-command which other
things, now that we have

00:49:58.270 --> 00:50:02.800 align:middle line:84%
this theory that we think, we
hope, is more or less reliable.

00:50:02.800 --> 00:50:04.300 align:middle line:84%
We know, when we
look at an anaphor,

00:50:04.300 --> 00:50:06.758 align:middle line:84%
it had better have an antecedent
that's close enough to it.

00:50:06.758 --> 00:50:09.220 align:middle line:84%
And we think that close
enough is more or less

00:50:09.220 --> 00:50:12.950 align:middle line:90%
within the same TP.

00:50:12.950 --> 00:50:14.390 align:middle line:84%
Forget it, now,
that I've done all

00:50:14.390 --> 00:50:15.557 align:middle line:90%
that talking about anaphors.

00:50:15.557 --> 00:50:17.580 align:middle line:84%
Forget about the
anaphors for a second.

00:50:17.580 --> 00:50:25.895 align:middle line:84%
If I say "John promised Mary to
leave," who's going to leave?

00:50:25.895 --> 00:50:26.520 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: John.

00:50:26.520 --> 00:50:27.540 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: John.

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:32.135 align:middle line:84%
And if I say "John told Mary to
leave," who's going to leave?

00:50:32.135 --> 00:50:32.760 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Mary.

00:50:32.760 --> 00:50:33.930 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Mary, yeah?

00:50:33.930 --> 00:50:38.370 align:middle line:84%
So the understood subjects
of these embedded clauses,

00:50:38.370 --> 00:50:41.400 align:middle line:84%
the PRO if there's
a PRO, is going

00:50:41.400 --> 00:50:49.590 align:middle line:84%
to refer to John when the
first verb is promised.

00:50:49.590 --> 00:50:53.730 align:middle line:84%
But it's going to
refer to Mary when--

00:50:53.730 --> 00:50:56.940 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to give
it a subscript, Mary--

00:50:56.940 --> 00:50:59.880 align:middle line:90%
the first verb is told, yeah?

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:02.580 align:middle line:84%
So let me do this
the easy way first.

00:51:02.580 --> 00:51:07.140 align:middle line:84%
If there is such a thing as PRO,
we ask ourself, these anaphors,

00:51:07.140 --> 00:51:10.050 align:middle line:84%
they need to get bound
within the smallest

00:51:10.050 --> 00:51:12.960 align:middle line:90%
TP that contains the anaphor.

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:15.330 align:middle line:84%
And the smallest TP that
contains the anaphor is

00:51:15.330 --> 00:51:19.820 align:middle line:90%
going to be this, yeah?

00:51:19.820 --> 00:51:24.410 align:middle line:84%
And so when we ask
ourself "Is the anaphor

00:51:24.410 --> 00:51:28.230 align:middle line:84%
bound by a noun phrase
that's close enough to it?"

00:51:28.230 --> 00:51:30.410 align:middle line:90%
well, the answer is yes here.

00:51:30.410 --> 00:51:33.560 align:middle line:84%
Because "himself" gets to be
bound by that PRO, "John."

00:51:33.560 --> 00:51:34.910 align:middle line:90%
That refers to John.

00:51:34.910 --> 00:51:37.260 align:middle line:90%
And so "himself" can be John.

00:51:37.260 --> 00:51:39.260 align:middle line:84%
It can't be "herself"
because the PRO is "John."

00:51:39.260 --> 00:51:43.700 align:middle line:84%
It's not "Mary," whereas, when
the higher verb is "told,"

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:45.140 align:middle line:90%
then the PRO is "Mary."

00:51:45.140 --> 00:51:47.090 align:middle line:84%
And so now "himself" is
bad because "himself"

00:51:47.090 --> 00:51:47.990 align:middle line:90%
can't refer to Mary.

00:51:47.990 --> 00:51:49.370 align:middle line:90%
And "herself" is good.

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:51.380 align:middle line:90%
It can get bound by "Mary."

00:51:51.380 --> 00:51:53.330 align:middle line:84%
So we can capture
all of these facts

00:51:53.330 --> 00:51:55.700 align:middle line:84%
if we're willing to
posit the PRO, which

00:51:55.700 --> 00:51:57.410 align:middle line:84%
refers to who it
should refer to,

00:51:57.410 --> 00:52:00.800 align:middle line:84%
the person who's going
to do the defending.

00:52:00.800 --> 00:52:03.110 align:middle line:84%
That'll depend on what
the higher verb is.

00:52:03.110 --> 00:52:05.240 align:middle line:84%
Interesting fact, which
people work on-- what

00:52:05.240 --> 00:52:07.220 align:middle line:90%
determines who PRO refers to?

00:52:07.220 --> 00:52:11.390 align:middle line:84%
Why is it "John" for "promise,"
but "Mary" for "defend"?

00:52:11.390 --> 00:52:14.990 align:middle line:84%
It's what's called subject
control and object control.

00:52:14.990 --> 00:52:16.940 align:middle line:84%
But once we believe
in PRO, we have

00:52:16.940 --> 00:52:20.690 align:middle line:84%
a handle on why the anaphors
refer to who they refer to.

00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:22.340 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:52:22.340 --> 00:52:23.690 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's do this the hard way.

00:52:23.690 --> 00:52:25.250 align:middle line:84%
What if we didn't
believe in PRO?

00:52:25.250 --> 00:52:28.690 align:middle line:90%


00:52:28.690 --> 00:52:36.330 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we don't believe
in PRO, woo, what we want

00:52:36.330 --> 00:52:44.550 align:middle line:84%
is for this position to
be bindable by "John,"

00:52:44.550 --> 00:52:45.780 align:middle line:90%
but not by "Mary."

00:52:45.780 --> 00:52:49.890 align:middle line:90%


00:52:49.890 --> 00:52:54.750 align:middle line:84%
And we want this position
to be bindable by "Mary,"

00:52:54.750 --> 00:52:55.620 align:middle line:90%
but not by "John."

00:52:55.620 --> 00:52:58.530 align:middle line:90%


00:52:58.530 --> 00:53:01.765 align:middle line:84%
And this is going
to be hard, right?

00:53:01.765 --> 00:53:03.640 align:middle line:84%
The first one is going
to be especially hard.

00:53:03.640 --> 00:53:05.880 align:middle line:84%
So far, our conditions
just say, anaphors

00:53:05.880 --> 00:53:07.380 align:middle line:84%
have to get c-commanded
by something

00:53:07.380 --> 00:53:11.940 align:middle line:84%
that's close enough to them
and corefers with them.

00:53:11.940 --> 00:53:14.313 align:middle line:84%
But if "John" is close enough
to that anaphor position,

00:53:14.313 --> 00:53:15.480 align:middle line:90%
well, then surely "Mary" is.

00:53:15.480 --> 00:53:16.890 align:middle line:90%
"Mary" is closer.

00:53:16.890 --> 00:53:19.500 align:middle line:90%
There's no way to do that.

00:53:19.500 --> 00:53:22.830 align:middle line:84%
I'll have to come up with
something very complicated.

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:24.690 align:middle line:84%
And the second set
of examples is also

00:53:24.690 --> 00:53:30.660 align:middle line:84%
going to be kind of hard because
"Mary" is the object of "told."

00:53:30.660 --> 00:53:33.660 align:middle line:84%
So this anaphor will need to
have a binding domain that

00:53:33.660 --> 00:53:36.900 align:middle line:84%
gets up to the higher verb
phrase, a domain in which

00:53:36.900 --> 00:53:40.840 align:middle line:90%
its anaphor finds a binder.

00:53:40.840 --> 00:53:42.840 align:middle line:84%
It's going to need to
find a binder in a domain.

00:53:42.840 --> 00:53:45.630 align:middle line:84%
We have to say, OK, we
were wrong about TPs.

00:53:45.630 --> 00:53:48.510 align:middle line:84%
It's, like, the next
verb phrase or something.

00:53:48.510 --> 00:53:50.610 align:middle line:84%
The domain in which
it finds a binder

00:53:50.610 --> 00:53:53.845 align:middle line:84%
has to be this new special kind
of domain that doesn't seem

00:53:53.845 --> 00:53:54.970 align:middle line:90%
to be useful anywhere else.

00:53:54.970 --> 00:53:57.060 align:middle line:90%
It's a mess, basically.

00:53:57.060 --> 00:53:59.640 align:middle line:84%
And PRO gets us out
of the mess if we're

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:04.840 align:middle line:84%
willing to put PRO in the
places where we want to put it.

00:54:04.840 --> 00:54:08.790 align:middle line:84%
So one reason to be happy
about binding theory

00:54:08.790 --> 00:54:11.940 align:middle line:84%
is that it gives us a reason
to make a move that we might

00:54:11.940 --> 00:54:13.500 align:middle line:90%
have wanted to make anyway.

00:54:13.500 --> 00:54:16.950 align:middle line:84%
These infinitives
are acting like they

00:54:16.950 --> 00:54:19.530 align:middle line:90%
have subjects in two respects.

00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:22.470 align:middle line:84%
They are satisfying
EPP, first of all.

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:25.980 align:middle line:84%
And they are acting like
they have a subject that

00:54:25.980 --> 00:54:27.840 align:middle line:84%
corefers with somebody
in the higher clause,

00:54:27.840 --> 00:54:31.110 align:middle line:84%
either "John" or "Mary," for
purposes of binding theory.

00:54:31.110 --> 00:54:35.580 align:middle line:84%
Binding theory is simplest
if there is a pronoun there.

00:54:35.580 --> 00:54:40.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, minor problem, we
can't see a pronoun there.

00:54:40.510 --> 00:54:44.422 align:middle line:84%
But if we're willing to rely
on binding theory a little bit,

00:54:44.422 --> 00:54:46.380 align:middle line:84%
we can use binding theory
to convince ourselves

00:54:46.380 --> 00:54:47.910 align:middle line:84%
that the pronoun
actually is there.

00:54:47.910 --> 00:54:49.440 align:middle line:90%
It's just that we can't see it.

00:54:49.440 --> 00:54:52.140 align:middle line:84%
We have a new
detector for pronouns.

00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:54.185 align:middle line:90%
We can find them this way.

00:54:54.185 --> 00:54:55.060 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:54:55.060 --> 00:54:56.500 align:middle line:84%
Are all of you
appropriately skeptical?

00:54:56.500 --> 00:54:58.120 align:middle line:84%
Is anybody
inappropriately skeptical?

00:54:58.120 --> 00:55:01.315 align:middle line:84%
Do you have questions about
any of this chain of reasoning?

00:55:01.315 --> 00:55:03.370 align:middle line:84%
It's a reason to take
seriously the idea

00:55:03.370 --> 00:55:06.280 align:middle line:84%
that there are invisible
pronouns sometimes.

00:55:06.280 --> 00:55:08.710 align:middle line:84%
We talked about invisible
pronouns before, I think.

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:11.860 align:middle line:84%
I think I gave you
examples like I can

00:55:11.860 --> 00:55:18.300 align:middle line:90%
say "Defend yourself," yeah?

00:55:18.300 --> 00:55:20.488 align:middle line:90%


00:55:20.488 --> 00:55:22.780 align:middle line:84%
I think somebody said that
they were taught in school--

00:55:22.780 --> 00:55:24.960 align:middle line:84%
I can't remember who it
was-- that the subject

00:55:24.960 --> 00:55:28.080 align:middle line:84%
of an imperative is
understood as "you," that this

00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:29.760 align:middle line:90%
means "You defend yourself."

00:55:29.760 --> 00:55:31.320 align:middle line:84%
And one reason to
take that seriously

00:55:31.320 --> 00:55:33.510 align:middle line:84%
is that, yeah, an
anaphor here is

00:55:33.510 --> 00:55:35.220 align:middle line:84%
acting like there
is a subject around,

00:55:35.220 --> 00:55:38.340 align:middle line:84%
or at least another noun phrase
around, that refers to "you."

00:55:38.340 --> 00:55:40.380 align:middle line:84%
Because "Defend
yourself" is fine.

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:44.127 align:middle line:84%
But "Defend myself"--
no good, right?

00:55:44.127 --> 00:55:45.960 align:middle line:84%
No matter how much you
might want to say it,

00:55:45.960 --> 00:55:47.168 align:middle line:90%
that's not the way to say it.

00:55:47.168 --> 00:55:49.110 align:middle line:90%
You say "Defend me," no?

00:55:49.110 --> 00:55:50.780 align:middle line:84%
It's acting like the
subject is "you."

00:55:50.780 --> 00:55:52.043 align:middle line:90%
OK, you can't hear it.

00:55:52.043 --> 00:55:54.210 align:middle line:84%
You can't hear the "you,"
but there's a "you" there.

00:55:54.210 --> 00:55:57.120 align:middle line:84%
You can use these
anaphors to find it.

00:55:57.120 --> 00:55:59.370 align:middle line:84%
Same thing here--
in "John promised

00:55:59.370 --> 00:56:01.770 align:middle line:84%
Mary to defend himself,"
there's a pronoun

00:56:01.770 --> 00:56:03.060 align:middle line:90%
in the subject of "defend."

00:56:03.060 --> 00:56:07.740 align:middle line:84%
It's really "John promised
Mary him to defend himself."

00:56:07.740 --> 00:56:11.280 align:middle line:84%
And that "him," that PRO,
is referring back to "John."

00:56:11.280 --> 00:56:14.670 align:middle line:84%
In "John told Mary her
to defend herself,"

00:56:14.670 --> 00:56:18.310 align:middle line:84%
that "her" is referring
back to "Mary."

00:56:18.310 --> 00:56:21.842 align:middle line:90%
Binding theory tells us so.

00:56:21.842 --> 00:56:25.780 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, yeah, yeah-- slide
after slide after slide.

00:56:25.780 --> 00:56:28.210 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:56:28.210 --> 00:56:31.240 align:middle line:84%
OK, here's another reason to
be happy about binding theory,

00:56:31.240 --> 00:56:33.370 align:middle line:84%
another thing that
it teaches us,

00:56:33.370 --> 00:56:35.770 align:middle line:84%
a new argument for
something that I showed you

00:56:35.770 --> 00:56:37.450 align:middle line:90%
an argument for before.

00:56:37.450 --> 00:56:40.270 align:middle line:84%
And now, I will show you
a new argument, possibly

00:56:40.270 --> 00:56:41.080 align:middle line:90%
a simpler argument.

00:56:41.080 --> 00:56:43.790 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I should
start with this one.

00:56:43.790 --> 00:56:45.970 align:middle line:84%
"Which picture of himself
did John like best"?

00:56:45.970 --> 00:56:49.090 align:middle line:90%
First-- setup for the argument.

00:56:49.090 --> 00:56:53.140 align:middle line:84%
Observation, "John"
sure doesn't look

00:56:53.140 --> 00:56:56.600 align:middle line:90%
like it c-commands himself.

00:56:56.600 --> 00:56:59.660 align:middle line:84%
But "John" used to
c-command "himself."

00:56:59.660 --> 00:57:05.040 align:middle line:84%
And apparently, principle A can
be satisfied with nostalgia.

00:57:05.040 --> 00:57:07.400 align:middle line:84%
So it thinks back
to the happy days

00:57:07.400 --> 00:57:10.190 align:middle line:84%
when "John" used to c-command
"himself," back before you

00:57:10.190 --> 00:57:11.870 align:middle line:90%
did wh- movement.

00:57:11.870 --> 00:57:13.700 align:middle line:90%
And it's satisfied.

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:15.850 align:middle line:84%
Principle A, it's
easy going that way.

00:57:15.850 --> 00:57:18.320 align:middle line:84%
So this starts off
with "John liked

00:57:18.320 --> 00:57:19.958 align:middle line:90%
which picture of himself best?"

00:57:19.958 --> 00:57:21.500 align:middle line:84%
"Which picture of
himself" starts off

00:57:21.500 --> 00:57:22.760 align:middle line:90%
as the object of "like."

00:57:22.760 --> 00:57:25.550 align:middle line:90%
And it wh- moves.

00:57:25.550 --> 00:57:26.870 align:middle line:90%
It becomes the specifier of CP.

00:57:26.870 --> 00:57:29.350 align:middle line:84%
Shall I draw a tree for
that, or is that clear?

00:57:29.350 --> 00:57:29.850 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:57:29.850 --> 00:57:32.140 align:middle line:84%
Those were yes/no questions
with opposite answers.

00:57:32.140 --> 00:57:35.300 align:middle line:84%
Who would like me to
draw a tree for that?

00:57:35.300 --> 00:57:35.800 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:57:35.800 --> 00:57:38.380 align:middle line:90%
OK, let me draw a tree for that.

00:57:38.380 --> 00:57:41.410 align:middle line:90%
I'll hide these trees.

00:57:41.410 --> 00:57:46.090 align:middle line:90%
So-- stamp.

00:57:46.090 --> 00:57:51.520 align:middle line:90%


00:57:51.520 --> 00:57:52.680 align:middle line:90%
John-- yeah.

00:57:52.680 --> 00:57:57.525 align:middle line:90%


00:57:57.525 --> 00:57:58.400 align:middle line:90%
Here's a noun phrase.

00:57:58.400 --> 00:58:01.890 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to leave out "best"
just because we don't need it.

00:58:01.890 --> 00:58:05.570 align:middle line:84%
Here's your DP,
"which," and N bar,

00:58:05.570 --> 00:58:11.583 align:middle line:84%
"picture," prepositional
phrase, "of,"

00:58:11.583 --> 00:58:12.875 align:middle line:90%
and the noun phrase, "himself."

00:58:12.875 --> 00:58:15.490 align:middle line:90%


00:58:15.490 --> 00:58:18.530 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is just "John liked
which picture of himself."

00:58:18.530 --> 00:58:19.780 align:middle line:90%
We could put a "best" in here.

00:58:19.780 --> 00:58:20.860 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why
I left out "best."

00:58:20.860 --> 00:58:21.700 align:middle line:90%
We'll put it here.

00:58:21.700 --> 00:58:31.020 align:middle line:90%


00:58:31.020 --> 00:58:34.070 align:middle line:84%
There, so "John liked which
picture of himself best?"

00:58:34.070 --> 00:58:37.580 align:middle line:84%
And then wh- movement
will take this noun phrase

00:58:37.580 --> 00:58:42.140 align:middle line:84%
and move it to make it the
specifier of a CP, which

00:58:42.140 --> 00:58:44.370 align:middle line:90%
is up here.

00:58:44.370 --> 00:58:49.890 align:middle line:84%
So now, we have "which
picture of himself"

00:58:49.890 --> 00:58:51.690 align:middle line:90%
in the specifier of CP.

00:58:51.690 --> 00:58:56.893 align:middle line:84%
And "did" ends up here
because we've moved T to C

00:58:56.893 --> 00:58:58.560 align:middle line:84%
and done do- support,
which is something

00:58:58.560 --> 00:59:00.390 align:middle line:90%
that Faith made me talk about.

00:59:00.390 --> 00:59:02.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:59:02.610 --> 00:59:05.557 align:middle line:84%
So "Which picture of
himself did John like best?"

00:59:05.557 --> 00:59:07.140 align:middle line:84%
And "which picture
of himself"-- which

00:59:07.140 --> 00:59:09.270 align:middle line:84%
I'd spelled without
the final H, just to

00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:12.168 align:middle line:84%
give you an
additional challenge--

00:59:12.168 --> 00:59:13.710 align:middle line:84%
"which picture of
himself" starts off

00:59:13.710 --> 00:59:14.790 align:middle line:90%
as the sister of "like."

00:59:14.790 --> 00:59:17.770 align:middle line:84%
And wh- moves and becomes
the specifier of CP.

00:59:17.770 --> 00:59:18.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:59:18.570 --> 00:59:19.890 align:middle line:90%
That's the derivation for that.

00:59:19.890 --> 00:59:23.490 align:middle line:90%


00:59:23.490 --> 00:59:28.510 align:middle line:84%
So fact, which we're going
to have to get used to--

00:59:28.510 --> 00:59:30.910 align:middle line:84%
after we do the movement,
"John" no longer c-commands

00:59:30.910 --> 00:59:31.690 align:middle line:90%
"himself," right?

00:59:31.690 --> 00:59:32.815 align:middle line:90%
What does "John" c-command?

00:59:32.815 --> 00:59:35.890 align:middle line:84%
Well, we ask which
nodes dominate "John."

00:59:35.890 --> 00:59:37.250 align:middle line:90%
And it's these nodes.

00:59:37.250 --> 00:59:39.545 align:middle line:84%
So "John" c-commands its
sister and everything

00:59:39.545 --> 00:59:40.420 align:middle line:90%
its sister dominates.

00:59:40.420 --> 00:59:43.270 align:middle line:84%
So "John" c-commands "like
which picture of himself best."

00:59:43.270 --> 00:59:45.770 align:middle line:84%
But "which picture of himself"
has moved out of that domain.

00:59:45.770 --> 00:59:46.330 align:middle line:90%
It's up here.

00:59:46.330 --> 00:59:49.460 align:middle line:90%


00:59:49.460 --> 00:59:54.280 align:middle line:84%
Apparently, the
fact that "himself"

00:59:54.280 --> 00:59:55.990 align:middle line:84%
used to be down
here in a position

00:59:55.990 --> 00:59:59.513 align:middle line:84%
that "John" could
c-command is good enough.

00:59:59.513 --> 01:00:01.180 align:middle line:84%
It's a phenomenon
called reconstruction.

01:00:01.180 --> 01:00:05.770 align:middle line:84%
It's as though, when you
are evaluating things

01:00:05.770 --> 01:00:08.340 align:middle line:84%
for binding theory, you get to
rewind the tape a little bit

01:00:08.340 --> 01:00:09.430 align:middle line:90%
and think, oh, yeah.

01:00:09.430 --> 01:00:10.513 align:middle line:90%
This used to be over here.

01:00:10.513 --> 01:00:11.590 align:middle line:90%
That's good enough.

01:00:11.590 --> 01:00:15.820 align:middle line:84%
Various ways to talk about
that, but that's one.

01:00:15.820 --> 01:00:18.490 align:middle line:90%
So file that fact.

01:00:18.490 --> 01:00:19.480 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I just said that.

01:00:19.480 --> 01:00:19.980 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:00:19.980 --> 01:00:21.760 align:middle line:84%
This phenomenon is
called reconstruction.

01:00:21.760 --> 01:00:25.700 align:middle line:84%
You treat something which is
moved as though it hadn't.

01:00:25.700 --> 01:00:26.480 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:00:26.480 --> 01:00:29.900 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's think about longer,
more complicated questions,

01:00:29.900 --> 01:00:33.430 align:middle line:84%
like "Which picture did John
think that Mary bought?"

01:00:33.430 --> 01:00:37.810 align:middle line:84%
So "which picture"
has wh- moved, right?

01:00:37.810 --> 01:00:41.410 align:middle line:84%
But we talked about this
question about whether it

01:00:41.410 --> 01:00:44.710 align:middle line:84%
has moved once in
one mighty leap,

01:00:44.710 --> 01:00:46.480 align:middle line:84%
started off as
the object of "by"

01:00:46.480 --> 01:00:48.760 align:middle line:84%
and moved through all of
that intervening material

01:00:48.760 --> 01:00:51.760 align:middle line:84%
and became the specifier
of the higher CP,

01:00:51.760 --> 01:00:54.880 align:middle line:90%
or moved in a series of hops.

01:00:54.880 --> 01:00:57.940 align:middle line:84%
Notice that "John thinks
that Mary bought a picture"

01:00:57.940 --> 01:00:59.560 align:middle line:90%
has two CPs in it.

01:00:59.560 --> 01:01:01.420 align:middle line:84%
There's "that Mary
bought a picture."

01:01:01.420 --> 01:01:02.710 align:middle line:90%
That's the embedded clause.

01:01:02.710 --> 01:01:05.350 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
the matrix clause.

01:01:05.350 --> 01:01:07.505 align:middle line:90%
And we raised the question.

01:01:07.505 --> 01:01:09.130 align:middle line:84%
And I gave you all
of these Dinka facts

01:01:09.130 --> 01:01:12.610 align:middle line:84%
that were meant to get you to
take seriously the possibility

01:01:12.610 --> 01:01:15.550 align:middle line:84%
that the second picture
is the right one,

01:01:15.550 --> 01:01:17.890 align:middle line:84%
that movement is what is
called successive cyclic.

01:01:17.890 --> 01:01:19.570 align:middle line:90%
That is you move in short hops.

01:01:19.570 --> 01:01:21.670 align:middle line:90%
You don't move in mighty leaps.

01:01:21.670 --> 01:01:25.630 align:middle line:84%
So a wh- phrase, it's
not just that it likes

01:01:25.630 --> 01:01:27.190 align:middle line:90%
to move to the specifier of CP.

01:01:27.190 --> 01:01:29.350 align:middle line:84%
It likes to land in
every specifier of CP

01:01:29.350 --> 01:01:30.940 align:middle line:90%
that it can find along the way.

01:01:30.940 --> 01:01:32.650 align:middle line:84%
It moves in this
series of short hops.

01:01:32.650 --> 01:01:34.150 align:middle line:84%
And I gave you some
Dinka facts that

01:01:34.150 --> 01:01:37.600 align:middle line:84%
were meant to get you to take
that possibility seriously.

01:01:37.600 --> 01:01:40.540 align:middle line:84%
Here's another set of
facts that might also

01:01:40.540 --> 01:01:42.850 align:middle line:84%
get you to take that
possibility seriously.

01:01:42.850 --> 01:01:45.370 align:middle line:84%
Think about a sentence like,
"Which picture of himself

01:01:45.370 --> 01:01:48.640 align:middle line:84%
did John think
that Mary bought?"

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:50.080 align:middle line:90%
First of all, it's fine.

01:01:50.080 --> 01:01:51.220 align:middle line:90%
Do people agree?

01:01:51.220 --> 01:01:53.660 align:middle line:84%
So "himself" can
refer to "John."

01:01:53.660 --> 01:01:55.660 align:middle line:84%
All right, now, let's
think about why it's fine.

01:01:55.660 --> 01:01:58.600 align:middle line:90%


01:01:58.600 --> 01:02:00.730 align:middle line:84%
"Which picture of
himself" starts off

01:02:00.730 --> 01:02:03.990 align:middle line:84%
as the sister of "by," starts
off in the embedded clause.

01:02:03.990 --> 01:02:06.490 align:middle line:84%
And it ends up, well, in the
position where it's pronounced,

01:02:06.490 --> 01:02:07.990 align:middle line:90%
in the matrix clause.

01:02:07.990 --> 01:02:10.330 align:middle line:84%
Is "himself" bound by
"John" in the position

01:02:10.330 --> 01:02:12.940 align:middle line:90%
where it's pronounced?

01:02:12.940 --> 01:02:16.090 align:middle line:90%
The specifier of the highest TP?

01:02:16.090 --> 01:02:17.635 align:middle line:84%
Does "John" c-command
that position?

01:02:17.635 --> 01:02:20.860 align:middle line:90%


01:02:20.860 --> 01:02:21.940 align:middle line:90%
No, right?

01:02:21.940 --> 01:02:23.260 align:middle line:90%
Shall I draw a tree for this?

01:02:23.260 --> 01:02:26.020 align:middle line:84%
Would that be a
good thing to do?

01:02:26.020 --> 01:02:27.932 align:middle line:90%
Let's draw a tree.

01:02:27.932 --> 01:02:30.390 align:middle line:84%
"Which picture of himself did
John think that Mary bought?"

01:02:30.390 --> 01:02:34.930 align:middle line:90%


01:02:34.930 --> 01:02:35.545 align:middle line:90%
Here's "John."

01:02:35.545 --> 01:02:40.140 align:middle line:90%


01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:44.770 align:middle line:90%
"Think," there's the CP.

01:02:44.770 --> 01:02:48.340 align:middle line:90%
"That," there's a TP.

01:02:48.340 --> 01:02:54.880 align:middle line:84%
"Mary," here's T. And here's
a VP and then "bought."

01:02:54.880 --> 01:02:56.980 align:middle line:84%
And so we start off
with "which picture,"

01:02:56.980 --> 01:02:59.563 align:middle line:84%
which I'm running out of room,
so I'm going to use a triangle.

01:02:59.563 --> 01:03:00.910 align:middle line:90%
Here's "which picture."

01:03:00.910 --> 01:03:02.410 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use a
triangle, and I'm

01:03:02.410 --> 01:03:04.478 align:middle line:84%
going to abbreviate
picture as "pic."

01:03:04.478 --> 01:03:05.770 align:middle line:90%
And the question we're asking--

01:03:05.770 --> 01:03:09.710 align:middle line:84%
and so here's the tree
we start off with, yeah?

01:03:09.710 --> 01:03:12.140 align:middle line:84%
Anybody wish to object to
any aspects of this tree?

01:03:12.140 --> 01:03:13.400 align:middle line:90%
Is this is OK?

01:03:13.400 --> 01:03:14.780 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:03:14.780 --> 01:03:17.480 align:middle line:84%
And the question we're asking
ourselves is, this noun phrase,

01:03:17.480 --> 01:03:21.210 align:middle line:90%
does it move just once to here?

01:03:21.210 --> 01:03:25.680 align:middle line:84%
So we have which
picture of himself.

01:03:25.680 --> 01:03:26.180 align:middle line:90%
[SNEEZE]

01:03:26.180 --> 01:03:28.885 align:middle line:90%
Bless you.

01:03:28.885 --> 01:03:33.000 align:middle line:84%
Does it move just once, or does
it stop along the way here?

01:03:33.000 --> 01:03:34.820 align:middle line:84%
That's the question
we're asking ourselves.

01:03:34.820 --> 01:03:36.570 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're going
to use binding theory

01:03:36.570 --> 01:03:39.480 align:middle line:90%
to come up with the answer.

01:03:39.480 --> 01:03:42.780 align:middle line:84%
Does "John" c-command
"himself" here?

01:03:42.780 --> 01:03:43.950 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:03:43.950 --> 01:03:45.660 align:middle line:84%
I just said "no"
before I drew the tree.

01:03:45.660 --> 01:03:47.077 align:middle line:84%
But now, that I've
drawn the tree,

01:03:47.077 --> 01:03:49.110 align:middle line:84%
you can see that the
answer is, in fact, no.

01:03:49.110 --> 01:03:52.020 align:middle line:84%
So "John" doesn't
c-command this position.

01:03:52.020 --> 01:03:56.240 align:middle line:84%
Does "John" c-command "himself"
when himself is down here?

01:03:56.240 --> 01:03:57.410 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, right?

01:03:57.410 --> 01:03:59.160 align:middle line:84%
So what's the c-command
domain for "John"?

01:03:59.160 --> 01:04:01.860 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's its sister, that
T bar, this T bar here,

01:04:01.860 --> 01:04:04.740 align:middle line:84%
and everything this T bar
dominates, so all of this.

01:04:04.740 --> 01:04:09.360 align:middle line:84%
So "John" c-commands
this position, "himself."

01:04:09.360 --> 01:04:10.560 align:middle line:90%
Is this just reconstruction?

01:04:10.560 --> 01:04:11.490 align:middle line:90%
Can "John" bind this?

01:04:11.490 --> 01:04:14.490 align:middle line:90%
No, it's too far away, right?

01:04:14.490 --> 01:04:16.548 align:middle line:90%
And so we can see that here.

01:04:16.548 --> 01:04:18.090 align:middle line:84%
You can't say "John
thought that Mary

01:04:18.090 --> 01:04:19.980 align:middle line:90%
bought a picture of himself."

01:04:19.980 --> 01:04:22.260 align:middle line:84%
So this position
is too far away.

01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:25.140 align:middle line:84%
And this position "John"
doesn't c-command.

01:04:25.140 --> 01:04:29.650 align:middle line:84%
If these are the only
positions, then we are doomed.

01:04:29.650 --> 01:04:33.510 align:middle line:84%
"Himself" can't be bound happily
in either of those positions,

01:04:33.510 --> 01:04:34.530 align:middle line:90%
doomed.

01:04:34.530 --> 01:04:38.130 align:middle line:84%
But if "which
picture of himself"

01:04:38.130 --> 01:04:44.910 align:middle line:84%
spent some time here, well,
then "John" c-commands "himself"

01:04:44.910 --> 01:04:47.790 align:middle line:84%
when "which picture of
himself" is here, right?

01:04:47.790 --> 01:04:51.360 align:middle line:84%
This is inside this T bar,
"John"'s sister, yeah?

01:04:51.360 --> 01:04:54.570 align:middle line:84%
It's dominated by all the same
nodes that dominate "John."

01:04:54.570 --> 01:05:00.540 align:middle line:84%
And it's not contained in
any TP that excludes "John."

01:05:00.540 --> 01:05:03.810 align:middle line:84%
Remember, our conditions on
how far away things can be

01:05:03.810 --> 01:05:05.963 align:middle line:84%
and still bind say
that anaphors need

01:05:05.963 --> 01:05:07.380 align:middle line:84%
to have binders
that are contained

01:05:07.380 --> 01:05:09.090 align:middle line:84%
in all the TPs
that contain them,

01:05:09.090 --> 01:05:12.190 align:middle line:90%
that aren't excluded by any TPs.

01:05:12.190 --> 01:05:16.020 align:middle line:84%
So if we do successive cyclic
movement, which Dinka already

01:05:16.020 --> 01:05:17.940 align:middle line:84%
told us that we
should, if movement

01:05:17.940 --> 01:05:21.450 align:middle line:84%
happens not in one big jump,
but in two small jumps,

01:05:21.450 --> 01:05:24.090 align:middle line:84%
then we have an account of this,
the fact that the sentence is

01:05:24.090 --> 01:05:25.110 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:05:25.110 --> 01:05:29.070 align:middle line:84%
The sentence is OK because
"himself," yes, it's been here.

01:05:29.070 --> 01:05:32.160 align:middle line:84%
Yes, it's up there, but
it's also been here.

01:05:32.160 --> 01:05:36.450 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that it spent time
here is why the sentence is OK.

01:05:36.450 --> 01:05:37.950 align:middle line:84%
Binding theory gives
us a new reason

01:05:37.950 --> 01:05:42.460 align:middle line:84%
to believe that, cool, so some
of the cool things binding

01:05:42.460 --> 01:05:43.210 align:middle line:90%
theory is for.

01:05:43.210 --> 01:05:43.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:05:43.710 --> 01:05:47.050 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So it's like you go
to multiple museums in one day,

01:05:47.050 --> 01:05:49.310 align:middle line:84%
they give you a little
stamp to get in the museum.

01:05:49.310 --> 01:05:49.750 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

01:05:49.750 --> 01:05:51.145 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And so at the end,
you look a little bit different.

01:05:51.145 --> 01:05:53.800 align:middle line:84%
You all have stamps
to show that you've

01:05:53.800 --> 01:05:56.310 align:middle line:90%
been to multiple museums.

01:05:56.310 --> 01:05:58.060 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes,
it's just like that.

01:05:58.060 --> 01:06:00.170 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:06:00.170 --> 01:06:00.910 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:06:00.910 --> 01:06:03.260 align:middle line:84%
So "himself" has been here,
and it's been up there.

01:06:03.260 --> 01:06:04.360 align:middle line:90%
But it's also been here.

01:06:04.360 --> 01:06:06.640 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that it was
here meant that it got to go

01:06:06.640 --> 01:06:10.860 align:middle line:84%
see the T-Rex skeleton
while it was here.

01:06:10.860 --> 01:06:12.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:06:12.570 --> 01:06:13.200 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

01:06:13.200 --> 01:06:16.020 align:middle line:90%


01:06:16.020 --> 01:06:18.120 align:middle line:84%
So movement is
successive-cyclic-- new reason

01:06:18.120 --> 01:06:18.900 align:middle line:90%
to believe that.

01:06:18.900 --> 01:06:22.420 align:middle line:90%


01:06:22.420 --> 01:06:25.210 align:middle line:84%
We have 15 minutes
left, and I don't

01:06:25.210 --> 01:06:27.340 align:middle line:84%
think I can do this next
thing in 15 minutes.

01:06:27.340 --> 01:06:28.720 align:middle line:90%
It involves guards.

01:06:28.720 --> 01:06:29.530 align:middle line:90%
No, maybe I can.

01:06:29.530 --> 01:06:31.360 align:middle line:84%
Let's start at
least, and then this

01:06:31.360 --> 01:06:34.580 align:middle line:84%
will give you something to think
about until we get to Thursday.

01:06:34.580 --> 01:06:36.178 align:middle line:90%
So consider this sentence.

01:06:36.178 --> 01:06:37.720 align:middle line:84%
"Two guards seem to
me to be standing

01:06:37.720 --> 01:06:39.880 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building."

01:06:39.880 --> 01:06:42.310 align:middle line:90%
Is that ambiguous?

01:06:42.310 --> 01:06:44.350 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's ambiguous in
a familiar way, right?

01:06:44.350 --> 01:06:48.870 align:middle line:84%
It either means each building
is guarded by two guards

01:06:48.870 --> 01:06:53.068 align:middle line:84%
as far as I can tell, or there
are two really weird guards

01:06:53.068 --> 01:06:54.860 align:middle line:84%
who are guarding all
the buildings at once.

01:06:54.860 --> 01:06:56.402 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it has those
readings at least.

01:06:56.402 --> 01:06:59.280 align:middle line:90%


01:06:59.280 --> 01:07:00.330 align:middle line:90%
How about this sentence?

01:07:00.330 --> 01:07:01.800 align:middle line:84%
"Two guards seem
to themselves"--

01:07:01.800 --> 01:07:03.540 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:07:03.540 --> 01:07:05.400 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

01:07:05.400 --> 01:07:07.320 align:middle line:84%
"Two guards seem to
themselves to be standing

01:07:07.320 --> 01:07:08.487 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building."

01:07:08.487 --> 01:07:11.550 align:middle line:90%


01:07:11.550 --> 01:07:14.070 align:middle line:90%
This is not ambiguous.

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:16.410 align:middle line:84%
This means that there
are two guards who

01:07:16.410 --> 01:07:19.320 align:middle line:90%
need to be taken off duty.

01:07:19.320 --> 01:07:21.840 align:middle line:84%
There are two guards who
are having hallucinations

01:07:21.840 --> 01:07:25.007 align:middle line:90%
of being very, very large.

01:07:25.007 --> 01:07:26.340 align:middle line:90%
That's what this sentence means.

01:07:26.340 --> 01:07:28.798 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't mean-- and there's
something else it could mean.

01:07:28.798 --> 01:07:32.618 align:middle line:84%
It could mean this
building has two guards who

01:07:32.618 --> 01:07:34.410 align:middle line:84%
think that they're
standing in front of it.

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:36.060 align:middle line:84%
That building has
two different guards

01:07:36.060 --> 01:07:38.018 align:middle line:84%
who think that they're
standing in front of it.

01:07:38.018 --> 01:07:39.813 align:middle line:84%
That building has
two other guards who

01:07:39.813 --> 01:07:40.980 align:middle line:90%
are standing in front of it.

01:07:40.980 --> 01:07:42.720 align:middle line:84%
In a sense, the reading
that it actually has

01:07:42.720 --> 01:07:43.710 align:middle line:90%
is the more reassuring one.

01:07:43.710 --> 01:07:45.793 align:middle line:84%
It just means that there
are two guards who really

01:07:45.793 --> 01:07:48.330 align:middle line:84%
need a psych profile,
whereas on the other reading

01:07:48.330 --> 01:07:50.850 align:middle line:84%
there are however many
buildings there are times two.

01:07:50.850 --> 01:07:54.040 align:middle line:84%
There are that many guards who
are in psychological trouble.

01:07:54.040 --> 01:07:56.880 align:middle line:84%
So it doesn't mean all of the
things that it could mean.

01:07:56.880 --> 01:08:00.990 align:middle line:84%
And you might be able to guess
the problem is themselves.

01:08:00.990 --> 01:08:04.200 align:middle line:84%
That's what's going to
prevent the other reading.

01:08:04.200 --> 01:08:06.720 align:middle line:84%
But for reasons of time,
let's let it prevent

01:08:06.720 --> 01:08:08.940 align:middle line:90%
the other reading on Thursday.

01:08:08.940 --> 01:08:10.500 align:middle line:84%
So come back on
Thursday, and I'll

01:08:10.500 --> 01:08:13.563 align:middle line:84%
finish this point, another thing
that binding theory is for.

01:08:13.563 --> 01:08:15.480 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk a little more
about reconstruction,

01:08:15.480 --> 01:08:16.290 align:middle line:90%
exactly what it is.

01:08:16.290 --> 01:08:17.335 align:middle line:90%
Because it's fascinating.

01:08:17.335 --> 01:08:19.710 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of really cool
work on trying to figure out

01:08:19.710 --> 01:08:20.989 align:middle line:90%
what the heck it means.

01:08:20.989 --> 01:08:21.689 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:08:21.689 --> 01:08:24.060 align:middle line:90%
Questions about anything else?

01:08:24.060 --> 01:08:25.050 align:middle line:90%
All right, good.

01:08:25.050 --> 01:08:29.990 align:middle line:84%
Go forth and think
about linguistics.

01:08:29.990 --> 01:08:35.000 align:middle line:90%